t axillary sentinel lymph nodes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to frozen section \u000a2. Primary tumor site left breast, short suture superior, long suture lateral, \u000ato mammo \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: \u000aWire, clip, mass in specimen. OR called. \u000a--SR \u000aO.R. CONSULTATION: \u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL LYMPH NODES  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000a3 negative lymph nodes. \u000aOR Consultation by: JEFFREY FRED KRANE, M.D., PH.D. \u000aResident: JUSTINE BARLETTA, M.D. \u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or \u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed \u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, "#1 left axillary sentinel lymph nodes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ," consists of 3 lymph nodes \u000a(1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm  and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.4cm ) with scant \u000aattached adipose tissue. The larger 2 nodes are bisected, and each node is \u000asubmitted separately as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID :  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  remnant, 2 frags, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID :  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  remnant, 2 frags, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID :  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  remnant, 1 frag, ESS \u000aPart B, "#2 primary tumor site left breast," consists of a breast excision \u000aspecimen (5.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 3.1cm ), with a localizing wire in place. The specimen \u000ais oriented by the surgeon with two sutures and has been previously inked by \u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph demonstrating the \u000awire, a mass, and the clip. There is an overlying skin ellipse (3.7 x 1.1cm ), \u000ain which no definitive scar can be identified. A firm white mass (1.1 x REMOVED_DATE x \u000a0.7cm ) is present 0.8cm from the posterior margin, 1.9cm from the superior \u000amargin, 1.8cm from the anterior margin, and >2cm from the inferior, medial \u000aand lateral margins. There is an area of fibrosis adjacent to the mass that \u000afocally extends to the posterior margin. The remainder of the breast parenchyma \u000ais composed of 100% adipose tissue. The mass is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : mass to closest posterior margin, 2 frags, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : mass to closest anterior and superior margins, 1 frag, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : additional superior and anterior margins, 2 frags, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : closest inferior margin with area of fibrosis, 2 frags, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : closest medial margin, 2 frags, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : closest lateral margin, 2 frags, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : skin, 2 frags, RSS \u000aBGL/jp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Briana Gleason M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
