VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DAVID I SOYBEL M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Ann K Folkins, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. LEFT BREAST, CENTRAL EXCISION FOR A MASS: \u000aIntraductal papillomata (multiple), associated with biopsy site \u000achanges, extending to the margin (see NOTE). \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia is present as a single focus \u000a(block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; levels examined). \u000aBenign breast tissue with biopsy site changes, apocrine metaplasia, \u000acystic changes, and hyperplasia. \u000aNOTE: \u000aThe patient had two (2) radiologic masses, one of which underwent core needle \u000abiopsy at HVMA and was reported to be a papilloma. The specimen radiograph of \u000athis specimen was reported to show a vague mass, not definitely correlating \u000awith the masses that were to be excised (see specimens below). There is no \u000amalignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, including \u000amargins. \u000aB. LEFT BREAST, SUPERIOR MARGIN, EXCISION FOR A MASS: \u000aIntraductal papilloma, associated with biopsy site changes. \u000aBenign breast tissue with apocrine metaplasia and hyperplasia. \u000aNOTE: \u000aThe specimen radiograph showed one of the targeted masses in this specimen. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aC. POSTERIOR MARGIN, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS: \u000aIntraductal papilloma associated with biopsy site changes. \u000aBenign breast tissue with hyperplasia, biopsy site changes, \u000asclerosing adenosis, and apocrine metaplasia. \u000aNOTE: \u000aThis specimen contained the localization wire and the second targeted mass. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester of the Breast Pathology Service. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 62 year-old female with intraductal papilloma with fibrosis (L) breast \u000a8/12/05 needle bx. \u000aOperation: Wire-directed excision (L) breast papilloma. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Intraductal papilloma, (L) breast. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. (L) breast mass - mammo. \u000aB/2. Superior margin - mammo. \u000aC/3. Posterior margin - mammo. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: \u000aSpecimen 1 - central - US shows mass. \u000aSpecimen 2 - superior margin - 0 US performed. \u000aSpecimen 3 - posterior margin - US shows mass. \u000aDr. Babagbemi \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient?s \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "#1. (L) breast mass", consists of one unoriented breast \u000aexcision specimen (5.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.8cm ) and one detached fragment of \u000alocalization wire. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a vague \u000adensity in the breast parenchyma near the edge of the specimen (0.9cm ). The \u000aspecimen is inked entirely blue and contains a poorly circumscribed white \u000afibrous firm area (1.6 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.6cm ), which grossly abuts the inked margin. \u000aIn addition, there is a well circumscribed firm yellow nodule (0.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm), which also grossly abuts the inked margin. The specimen consists of 80% \u000aadipose tissue and 20% fibrous tissue including the aforementioned firm areas. \u000aThe specimen is sectioned into eleven levels ( 0.3cm each level). The \u000aspecimen is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 1, 6 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 2 with fibrous area, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 3 with fibrous area, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 4 with fibrous area, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 5 with fibrous area, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 6 with fibrous area, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 7 with fibrous area, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 8 with firm nodule, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 9, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 10, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 11, 5 frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Superior margin", consists of one breast excision specimen \u000a(3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm ). The specimen is received unoriented and is inked \u000aentirely blue. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a vague \u000ahyperdense region on one end of the specimen (0.5cm ). The specimen contains a \u000asmall firm white area (0.4 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm ), which grossly abuts the inked \u000amargin. The remainder of the breast parenchyma consists of 95% adipose \u000atissue and 10% fibrous tissue. The specimen is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin with nodule, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin, 5 frags, ESS. \u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Posterior margin", consists of one unoriented breast \u000aexcision specimen (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.5cm ) with a needle localization wire in place \u000aand a separately received fragment of fibroadipose tissue (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.8cm ). \u000aAn accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a well-circumscribed white \u000ahyperdense mass (0.9cm in greatest dimension) with a needle localization wire \u000ain the center. The specimen contains a partially cystic hemorrhagic area in \u000athe region of the wire (0.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.1cm ), which grossly abuts the inked \u000amargin. In addition, there is a tan/red firm hemorrhagic area (0.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) in another region of the specimen. The specimen is sectioned in eleven \u000alevels, each of which measures 0.3cm in thickness. The specimen consists of \u000a95% adipose tissue and 5% fibrous tissue, and the separately received \u000afragment consists of grossly unremarkable fibroadipose tissue. The specimen is \u000aentirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Levels 1 and 2, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 3, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level REMOVED_DATE (1 frag each), with tip of wire, 5 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Levels REMOVED_DATE with nodule, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Level 11, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Separate fragment, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aAF/ajb \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Ann K Folkins M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on \u000a10/17/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
