, \u000aand a whipstitch designating the posterior margin. There is a needle \u000alocalization wire in place entering through the superior lateral aspect of the \u000aspecimen and extends through the medial inferior aspect of the specimen. The \u000aspecimen has been previously inked per pathology protocol by the surgeon \u000a(superior red, inferior green, lateral yellow, medial orange, anterior blue, \u000aand posterior black). The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to \u000ainferior. The cut surface has tan/yellow homogenous focal areas and dense \u000awhite fibrous tissue adjacent to the needle localization wire at the inferior \u000amedial aspect of the specimen. The fibrous tissue constitutes approximately \u000a80% of the breast parenchyma. No definitive lesions or areas of disruption are \u000agrossly identified. The entire specimen is submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Sequential cross section superior to inferior. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 1 frag each, ESS. Blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to include radiographed areas of \u000acalcification. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Inferior margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000aSEK/elw \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michael W Bennett M.B., B.C.H., Electronically signed on \u000a4/2/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
