VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Michael A. Seidman, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, MRI GUIDED DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A PRIOR CORE NEEDLE \u000aBIOPSY SITE: \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform type (intermediate nuclear grade) \u000awith necrosis, is present as a 0.4cm focus in one of 17 blocks (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aThe DCIS is close to the prior core needle biopsy site. \u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU, extensive, is present in 8 blocks and \u000aextensively involves sclerosing adenosis. The LCIS is positive for estrogen \u000aand progesterone receptors and negative for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (1+). The LCIS is \u000anegative for e-cadherin. Immunoperoxidase studies for p63 and cytokeratin \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID / REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  on four blocks confirm the absence of invasive carcinoma. \u000aColumnar cell change with flat atypia is extensively present in 6 \u000ablocks. \u000aDCIS is < 0.1cm from the medial margin (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). A focus of severely \u000aatypical ductal hyperplasia is present at the inferior margin (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). The \u000aremaining margins are free of DCIS by > 0.3cm  \u000aNote: The patient had an irregular 2cm enhancing mass by MRI. A core \u000aneedle biopsy showed LCIS and columnar cell change ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). In this \u000aexcision, the very extensive LCIS in sclerosing adenosis may correspond to \u000athe enhancing mass. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies for ER, PR, and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu will be performed on \u000athe DCIS and reported in an addendum. \u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition) Tis NX. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 57 year old postmenopausal female with a history of  REMOVED_CASE_ID  R core biopsy \u000arevealing ALH. REMOVED_DATE bilateral MRI demonstrates bilateral findings with \u000abiopsy recommended. REMOVED_DATE bilateral core biopsy revealed R LCIS and L \u000acolumnar cell change. R breast excision is recommended. \u000aOperation: R breast MRI-guided wire localized biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: Not provided. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R breast MRI. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) right breast tissue - short stitch/superior, long stitch/lateral, whip \u000astitch/chest wall margin (FS Room - mammo). \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aSpecimen contains clip and two (2) intact wires. Called to OR. Dr. Lizer. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name, \u000amedical record number, and "#1. Right breast tissue", and consists of a partial \u000amastectomy specimen (4.6cm superior to inferior, 4.1cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm anterior to posterior) with two localization wires intact, and three \u000astitches (as per surgeon, short stitch/superior, long stitch/lateral, \u000awhip-stitch/chest wall margin). The specimen is received inked as per \u000apathology protocol by the surgeon for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. The specimen radiograph is received and identifies both \u000alocalization wires as well as prior biopsy clip. The biopsy clip is readily \u000aidentified and removed. The specimen is predominantly fibrous without discrete \u000alesions identified. The specimen is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Biopsy site, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remaining breast tissue, 1 to multi frags each, ESS. \u000aSEI/lp \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 15,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05:36:28PM \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for DCIS (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) EQUIVOCAL (2+) \u000aNote: Normal epithelium is appropriately reactive for estrogen and progesterone \u000areceptors. \u000aNote: FISH analysis for amplification of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu is pending. \u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following \u000acriteria: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000acarcinoma is reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a \u000aportion of the membrane is positive. \u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of \u000atumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in < 30% \u000aof tumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of \u000atumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity. \u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests \u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance \u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the \u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the \u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such \u000aclearance or approval is not necessary. \u000aAddendum #1 by Amy Ly M.D., Electronically signed on Tuesday REMOVED_MONTH 20, \u000a REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 03:50:52PM \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (10/23/09) \u000aFISH was performed by Clarient using the PathVysion HER-2 DNA Probe Kit with \u000athe following (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aResults: NOT AMPLIFIED \u000aHER-2/CEP-17 ratio = REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average of HER-2 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average centromere 17 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aREFERENCE RANGE: \u000aNOT AMPLIFIED HER-2/CEP-17 ratio < REMOVED_DATE \u000aEQUIVOCAL HER-2/CEP-17 ratio REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE \u000aAMPLIFIED  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /CEP-17 ratio > REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #2 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aFriday REMOVED_MONTH 23,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 03:41:32PM \u000a[report_end] 
