VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Peter M Sadow, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST EXCISION WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR A CLIP: \u000aNo carcinoma or atypia identified. \u000aBiopsy site changes are present. \u000aA tiny focus of early calcification is identified in \u000aassociation with benign breast epithelium (slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), but significant \u000acalcifications are not present in the sections of the specimen or in \u000athe specimen radiograph. \u000aSkin shows no significant pathologic change. \u000aThe specimen is submitted in toto for histological evaluation and all \u000amargins examined. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Micro calcification of left breast, core Bx DCIS. \u000aOperation: None given. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) L breast tissue --> mammo. \u000aRADIOLOGY: Wire clip in specimen. OR called/SR. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen, Part A, is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit \u000anumber, and "L breast tissue", and consists of a left breast excision specimen \u000athat measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3.1cm with a wire protruding through a pink skin \u000aellipse that measures REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm  The skin ellipse contains no obvious scar. \u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aPosterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. On section, the specimen is mostly white, fibrous tissue with \u000avery little fat. There are focal areas of hemorrhage and also these likely \u000alocalized to the biopsy site. There is a firm nodule adjacent to the biopsy \u000asite that measures 1.2cm in greatest dimension with central hemorrhage. The \u000anodule is not distinguished from the surrounding fibrous tissue. \u000aA clip was detected by the radiograph, submitted with the specimen. Following \u000asectioning, the specimen is re-radiographed by the pathology resident to reveal \u000athe clip. Due to the fibrotic and hemorrhagic nature of the excision specimen \u000awith such extensive fibrous areas involving margins, the specimen is entirely \u000asubmitted. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast excision, REMOVED_DATE frags in each, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast slice with area of clip, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast with overlying skin ellipse, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of breast excision specimen, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS. \u000aPES/mmm \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Peter M Sadow M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
