VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aPathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE \u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D. \u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D. \u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A LOBULATED MASS AND A CLIP MARKING A \u000aPRIOR CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY: \u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA involving a sclerosing papilloma \u000a(block 10; levels examined) (see note). An immunohistochemical study for \u000ap63 reveals basally located myoepithelial cells throughout the lesion, as \u000awell as scattered cells within the areas of hyperplasia. The entire \u000asclerosing papilloma measures 0.8cm in size. \u000aThe closest margin is lateral which is < 0.1cm from the sclerosing \u000apapilloma. \u000aThe prior core needle biopsy site is present associated with the \u000asclerosing papilloma. \u000aNote: This lesion is evaluated in conjunction with the prior core needle \u000abiopsy (BWH consultation  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) and both show similar features. \u000aThe hyperplasia is difficult to evaluate due to the involvement of the \u000apapilloma. Areas of the hyperplasia demonstrate regular cribriform \u000aspaces. Some of the spaces are filled with mucin or basement membrane \u000amaterial, suggestive of mucinous and collagenous spherulosis. This is \u000asupported by the p63 study which suggests that the epithelial \u000aproliferation is made up of both luminal and myoepithelial cells. \u000aHowever, other spaces appear empty, more typical of DCIS. \u000aThe differential diagnosis includes a low grade DCIS versus ADH involving \u000aa papilloma. The latter diagnosis is favored due to the lack of \u000adefinitive areas of DCIS outside the area of this focal lesion. However, \u000athe lesion is very close to the margin. Re-excision may be helpful to \u000aexclude an adjacent area of DCIS. \u000aDr. James Connolly reviewed selected slides and concurs with the \u000ainterpretation of this lesion as severely atypical ductal hyperplasia. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Status post core biopsy REMOVED_DATE  ? DCIS. \u000aOperation: Left wire localized partial mastectomy. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Left wire localized partial mastectomy. \u000aRadiology report: \u000aMass and clip are in specimen. Dr. S. Durfee \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by O'CONNELL,FIONNUALA,MB,BCH,BAO \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, \u000aand "#1, left wire localized partial mastectomy", and consists of a single \u000afragment of pink-tan fibroadipose tissue, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.0cm  The specimen has \u000aa localization wire protruding from it. The specimen is inked per pathology \u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; \u000asuperior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The \u000ainterior of the specimen is mostly fat, with scant fibrous tissue. A core \u000aneedle biopsy site spans the length of the specimen from the anterior to the \u000aposterior aspect. No obvious mass is noted adjacent to the core biopsy site or \u000awithin any other area of the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned and \u000aentirely submitted. The specimen is submitted with an accompanying specimen \u000aradiograph, which demonstrates the presence of a localizing wire, mass and a \u000aclip. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  anterior, ESS. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  posterior, ESS. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of the specimen, anterior to posterior, ESS. \u000afsd \u000aReports to: KAELIN, CAROLYN, MARY, M.PH. M.D. \u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE \u000a[report_end] 
