 in two parts, each labeled with the \u000apatient's name, unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "left breast site #1", consists of three white cassettes with \u000agreen lids containing multiple cylindrical fibrofatty focally hemorrhagic soft \u000atissues (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm in aggregate), which are submitted in toto. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Contents of white cassettes with green lids, multi frags in each, \u000aESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "left breast site #2", contains four white cassettes with blue \u000alids with multiple cylindrical focally hemorrhagic fibrofatty soft tissues (3.0 \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm in aggregate), which are submitted in toto. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Contents of white cassettes with blue lid, multi frags in each, \u000aESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aJC/ag \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Friday \u000aFebruary 13,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 12:17:27PM \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (2/19/09): \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) NEGATIVE (1+) \u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for \u000aprogesterone receptors. \u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following \u000acriteria: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not \u000ayet been determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor \u000acells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but \u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive. \u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in \u000a>10% of tumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in \u000a>30% of tumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational \u000ause only. \u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday \u000aFebruary 19,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 07:44:28PM \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aAn immunoperoxidase study for e-cadherin is performed on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Although \u000athe invasive carcinoma has lobular morphology, the carcinoma is moderately \u000aimmunoreactive. Therefore, this is an unusual lobular carcinoma with \u000ae-cadherin expression. Some of the in situ carcinoma is also e-cadherin \u000apositive. Other cells have weak or absent expression. Final classification of \u000aboth components should be made on the excisional specimen. \u000aAddendum #2 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Wednesday \u000aREMOVED_MONTH 22,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 07:24:18PM \u000a[report_end] 
