ACCESSION_ID : Fibroadipose tissue with white nodularity, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left breast tissue, two wires, two microcalcifications - \u000ashort suture inferior, long suture medial", consists of a fragment of \u000afibroadipose tissue (2.4cm superior to inferior, 4.0cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm anterior to posterior). There are two orienting sutures: a short suture \u000adesignating inferior and a long suture designating medial per surgeon. The \u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. There are two wires entering the specimen with wire #1 entering \u000athe lateral aspect going towards the anterior aspect and wire #2 entering the \u000asuperior aspect going to the posterior aspect. Wire #1 is associated with a \u000aclip. A radiograph accompanies the specimen showing wire #1 associated with \u000aclip and microcalcifications in wire #2 associated with microcalcifications. \u000aThe specimen is sectioned into seven slices going from anterior to posterior \u000awith slice #1 as anterior and slice #7 as posterior. A clip is identified \u000aassociated with wire #1 and with surrounding hemorrhage and white fibrous \u000atissue (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.6cm ) and located from margins as follows: 1.3cm \u000asuperior, 0.5cm inferior, 3.5cm medial, abutting lateral, 3.2cm anterior, \u000aand abutting posterior. No lesions are grossly associated with wire #2. The \u000aspecimen is entirely submitted for histologic evaluation. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #1 with anterior margin perpendicular, 4 frags in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and 3 \u000afrags in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #4, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #5, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #6, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #7 with posterior margin perpendicular, 3 frags in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and 4 \u000afrags in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , ESS. \u000aNote: Hemorrhagic/white fibrous area associated with clip is in slices #5 \u000athrough #7 and in cassettes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . The clip is in micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Left breast tissue lateral to lower clip area", consists \u000aof an unoriented fragment of fibroadipose tissue (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.6cm ). The \u000aouter aspect of the specimen is inked blue. The specimen is entirely submitted \u000afor histologic evaluation. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Fibroadipose tissue sectioned, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER: BG-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aGL/am \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aThursday May 14,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 06:20:55PM \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the \u000aspecimen, including margins. \u000aAddendum #1 by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 10,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 01:37:24PM \u000a[report_end] 
