VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Elgida Radoncipi, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. LEFT BREAST TISSUE: \u000aBreast parenchyma with atypical lobular hyperplasia (best seen in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and \u000ahighlighted by immunohistochemistry for E-cadherin), calcifications in the \u000asetting of benign breast tissue, and columnar cell changes. \u000aMargins were evaluated for diagnosis. \u000aThe immunohistochemical profile supports the above diagnosis. \u000aB. LEFT BREAST TISSUE: \u000aBreast parenchyma with atypical lobular hyperplasia with Pagetoid spread (best \u000aseen in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and highlighted by immunohistochemistry for E-cadherin), \u000acalcifications in the setting of benign breast tissue, and columnar cell \u000achanges. \u000aMargins were evaluated for diagnosis. \u000aThe immunohistochemical profile supports the above diagnosis. \u000aNOTE: \u000aDr. S. Lester, Breast Pathology Service, also reviewed the case and concurs. \u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests \u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance \u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the \u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the \u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such \u000aclearance or approval is not necessary. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 68-year-old female whose REMOVED_DATE mammogram demonstrates left breast \u000aupper outer quadrant 2cm faint punctate and amorphous calcifications for which \u000awire localization and excision is recommended. \u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: Not given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Left breast tissue - short suture superior, long lateral, whipstitch on \u000adeep margin. \u000aB/2. Left breast posterior shave margin, whipstitch on chest wall margin. \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aCalcifications and intact wire are in the specimen. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname, unit number. \u000aPart A, "left breast tissue", consists of an irregular fragment of fibroadipose \u000atissue (5.0cm M - L x REMOVED_DATE S - I x 1.5cm A - P) with a short suture \u000adesignating superior, a long suture designating lateral, and a whipstitch \u000adesignating the deep margin per surgeon. The specimen is inked per pathology \u000aprotocol. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the \u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen radiograph denotes \u000aa radiograph, which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits \u000athrough the anterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is serially \u000asectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit an ill-defined tan-yellow firm \u000afocally hemorrhagic (due to needle localization) mass (0.9 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.6cm ) \u000alocated 2.0cm from the medial margin, 2.1cm from the lateral margin, 0.4cm \u000afrom the anterior margin, 0.6cm from the inferior margin, 0.6cm from the \u000aposterior margin and 4.0cm from the superior margin. The needle-localization \u000awire traverses this area. The remaining breast parenchyma is comprised of 15% \u000awhite fibrous tissue to 85% yellow lobulated adipose tissue; the entire \u000aspecimen is submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular medial margin, 2 frags, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Body, sequentially submitted from medial to lateral to include \u000aareas denoted by needle-localization wire ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 1 frag in each, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular lateral margin, 2 frags, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of specimen, multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, "left breast posterior shave margin", consists of an irregular fragment \u000aof fibroadipose tissue (8.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ) with a whipstitch designating the \u000achest wall margin per surgeon (inked black). The opposing surface is inked \u000ablue per prosector. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit 75% white \u000afibrous tissue to 25% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. No masses or lesions \u000aare grossly appreciated. The entire specimen was submitted for microscopic \u000aexamination. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 1 frag in each, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000ajnc/elw \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on Friday \u000aAugust 28,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05:17:25PM \u000a[report_end] 
