ted by sutures designating superior (short suture), \u000alateral (long suture), and chest wall (whip stitch) margins. The specimen is \u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange \u000a(inked by surgeon). Localizing wire enters inferior/posterior surface and \u000aexits anterior/superior surface. The specimen is received with an accompanying \u000aradiograph demonstrating a localizing wire in place within the specimen, which \u000aexhibits peripheral focal calcifications. The specimen is serially sectioned \u000afrom medial to lateral, revealing ____ tan/white fibrous tissue (at least 90%), \u000awithout palpable or visible masses, lesions or other abnormalities grossly. \u000aEntire specimen is sequentially submitted from medial to lateral, with \u000aperpendicular medial margin in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , perpendicular lateral margin in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Multi frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000adb\u005cmgd\u005cpab \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Friday May \u000a08,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05:59:30PM \u000a[report_end] 
