VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: GREGORY J KANE M.D. \u000aCASE: BG-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Justine A Barletta, M.D. \u000aPathologist: George L Mutter, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. SPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST TISSUE": \u000aBenign breast tissue with apocrine cysts and fibrosis (cystic disease). \u000aMicrocalcifications in association with benign epithelium. \u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy. \u000aB. SPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT FIBROCYSTIC BREAST TISSUE, LATERAL TO SPECIMEN #1": \u000aBenign breast tissue with apocrine cysts and fibrosis (cystic disease). \u000aMicrocalcifications in association with benign epithelium. \u000aEpithelial hyperplasia, benign. \u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Slightly suspicious cluster of cysts & small lesion in left breast. \u000aPast history of fibrocystic disease left breast. Ultrasound left breast, \u000arecommended biopsy. \u000aOperation: Left breast resection (wire). \u000aOperative Findings: Multiple cystic structure. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Fibrocystic disease. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000a1. Left breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral \u000a2. Left fibrocystic breast tissue, lateral to specimen \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname, unit number. \u000aPart A, "left breast tissue; short stitch superior, long stitch lateral", \u000aconsists of a portion of yellow lobulated fibroadipose tissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with two orienting sutures (short stitch superior, long stitch lateral) and \u000aa needle-localization wire in place (entering at the lateral aspect). The \u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral, \u000aexhibiting an ill-defined, white, firm, cystic mass (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm ), which \u000ais traversed by the needle-localization wire. The cystic mass abuts the \u000aposterior, anterior, and superior margins, comes to within 0.8cm of the \u000ainferior margin, 0.7cm of the medial margin, and 1.1cm of the lateral \u000amargins. The accompanying radiograph confirms the presence of a density with a \u000aneedle-localization wire in place. Note: An additional free floating wire \u000a(2.0cm in length) is present within the container. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial aspect of the breast specimen to include lesion, 4 frags, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral portion of breast tissue, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, "left fibrocystic breast tissue, lateral to specimen #1", consists of \u000athree fragments of unoriented yellow/white fibroadipose soft tissue (ranging \u000afrom REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.7cm up to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ). Each fragment is inked \u000ablack and sectioned, exhibiting approximately 95% tan/white dense, focally \u000acystic stroma and 5% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen was \u000aaccompanied by a radiograph exhibiting two fragments of tissue with no \u000alocalized densities or lesions specified by radiology. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Small fragment of fibroadipose tissue, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Largest fragment of fibroadipose tissue (trisected), 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Fragment fibroadipose tissue, bisected, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aKG:lc \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by George L Mutter M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
