VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: GREGORY J KANE M.D. \u000aCASE: BG-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.; Eleanor Y. Chen, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Andrea L Richardson, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aINVASIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA, well differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson \u000agrade I/III), single focus grossly measuring 0.9cm in size. \u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present. \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types (low nuclear grade) is \u000apresent in 2 of 10 total blocks. DCIS is associated with calcifications and \u000anecrosis. \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present, with pagetoid spread. \u000aInvasive carcinoma is present at the inferior resection margin (separate margin \u000aexcision performed; see below). Invasive carcinoma is present 0.2cm from the \u000aanterior, and >0.3cm from the superior, posterior, medial, and lateral \u000aresection margins. \u000aDCIS is present <0.1cm from the anterior (focal, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) and medial (focal, \u000ablock  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) resection margins. DCIS is >0.3cm from the posterior, lateral, \u000asuperior, and inferior resection margins. \u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition):  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID b NX. \u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): Pending. \u000aB. RIGHT BREAST, MARGIN EXCISION, "TISSUE INFERIOR TO SPECIMEN #1": \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid type (low nuclear grade) is present in 2 of 3 \u000atotal blocks. No calcifications or necrosis is associated with DCIS. \u000aDCIS is present >0.3cm from the new (blue inked) margin. \u000aC. RIGHT BREAST, MARGIN EXCISION, "TISSUE SUPERIOR TO SPECIMEN #1": \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present. \u000aNo carcinoma is present, including evaluation of specimen margins. \u000aD. RIGHT BREAST, MARGIN EXCISION, "TISSUE SUPERIOR TO SPECIMEN #3": \u000aBreast tissue with no carcinoma present, including evaluation of margins. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Patient history cancer of right breast with DCIS,  REMOVED_CASE_ID , 1.0cm \u000ainfilt ductal cancer + DCIS, ? Second process of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , now has \u000amicrocalcification in right breast. \u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized lumpectomy. \u000aOperative Findings: Nodularity near chest wall above nipple, right breast. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: PreOp: Right breast calcifications. \u000aPostOp: Nodule in specimen #1. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Right breast tissue (short suture: Medial; long suture: Inferior; long \u000a"S" suture: Deep). Mammo. \u000aB/2. Tissue inferior to specimen #1, suture on the aspect closest to specimen \u000a#1. Mammo. \u000aC/3. Tissue superior to specimen #1 with local wire, suture on aspect closest \u000ato the specimen #1. Mammo. \u000aD/4. Breast tissue superior to the specimen (perm), 3 - unoriented. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: \u000aSpecimen #1: Dense breast tissue and calcifications are seen. \u000aSpecimen #2: Calcifications are seen within the specimen. \u000aSpecimen #3: Calcifications and a fragment of hookwire are seen. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in four parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname, and unit number. \u000aPart A, "right breast tissue (short suture medial, long suture inferior, long S \u000asuture deep)", consists of a REMOVED_DATE (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE (inferior to \u000asuperior) x REMOVED_DATE (inferior to posterior)cm breast biopsy with a short suture \u000amarking the medial margin, a long suture marking the inferior margin and S \u000asuture marking the deep/posterior margin. Please note that the specimen is \u000areceived with no localizing wires and accompanying specimen radiograph shows \u000acalcifications. The specimen is inked for the evaluation of margins: \u000aAnterior/blue, posterior/black, medial/orange, lateral/yellow, superior/red, \u000aand inferior/green. The tissue consists of approximately 40% dense fibrous \u000atissue with an area of firmness (1.0cm in greatest dimension), abutting the \u000amedial margin. No obvious masses are identified. The specimen is serially \u000asectioned from lateral to medial and entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections from medial to lateral, multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, "tissue inferior to specimen #1, suture on the aspect closest to \u000aspecimen #1", consists of a fragment of breast tissue (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ) with \u000aa suture designating the aspect closest to the specimen #1. For orientation \u000athe suture designated aspect is inked black and the side opposite from it is \u000ainked blue. The breast parenchyma consists of approximately 40% fibrous \u000atissue. The specimen is serially sectioned and entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections, 6 frags, ESS. \u000aPart C, "tissue superior to specimen #1 with local wire, suture on aspect \u000aclosest to the specimen #1", consists of a fragment of breast tissue (3.3 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax 0.7cm ) with a suture designating aspect closest to the specimen #1. For \u000aorientation the suture S designated aspect is inked black and the side opposite \u000afrom it is inked blue. The breast tissue consists of 20-30% fibrous tissue \u000awith no obvious gross lesions. The specimen is serially sectioned and entirely \u000asubmitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections, multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart D, "breast tissue superior to the specimen #3", consists of five fragments \u000aof breast tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.8cm in aggregate. The breast tissue \u000ais composed of mostly adipose tissue and is focally hemorrhagic. The entire \u000aspecimen is sectioned and entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Entire specimen sectioned, multi frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aEYC:jjk \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a1/19/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (1/24/07): \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for INVASIVE carcinoma (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) NEGATIVE (0) \u000aInterpretation: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor \u000acells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only \u000aa portion of the membrane is positive. \u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% \u000aof tumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in \u000a>10% of tumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational \u000ause only. \u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (3/19/07): \u000aImmunohistochemistry for E-cadherin confirms absence of membrane \u000aimmunoreactivity in the invasive carcinoma supporting lobular phenotype. \u000aAddendum #2 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
