VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Lynette M Sholl, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, SHORT/SUPERIOR, LONG/LATERAL, WHIP \u000aSTITCH ON DEEP MARGIN, SCAR/ANTERIOR": \u000aSingle microscopic focus of atypical ductal hyperplasia, present < REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the medial margin (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aBiopsy site changes with scar present at the inferior margin. \u000aSkin with scar. \u000aNOTE: The prior case ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) was reviewed and the morphology of ADH \u000ain this specimen is similar to that of the prior specimen. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 64 year old female with history of severe ADH. REMOVED_DATE - right breast \u000acore biopsy - extensive atypical lobular hyperplasia; REMOVED_DATE - right breast \u000awire loc, severely ADH; mammo REMOVED_DATE - Boston Imaging - RUO - calcs; REMOVED_DATE - \u000aright breast, scattered punctate cals in UO breast, prob benign. \u000aOperation: Right breast MRI-directed wire localized biopsy. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal right breast MRI. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) right breast tissue, short/superior, long/lateral, whip stitch on deep \u000amargin, scar/anterior. \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aDr. Raza spoke with Dr. Rhei with results. UM. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number, \u000aand "#1 - right breast tissue, short=superior, long=lateral, whip stitch on \u000adeep margin (scar anterior)", and consists of a breast excision specimen (7.3 x \u000a3.7 x 0.8cm ) with a long stitch in place (designated lateral), a whip stitch \u000ain place (designated deep margin). A short stitch is not identified within the \u000aspecimen. There is a white/tan skin ellipse (1.6 x 0.4cm ) with a 0.7cm in \u000alength well-healed scar that is centrally placed. The specimen is received \u000awith a localizing wire in place, entering through the superior aspect and \u000aexiting through the posterior aspect, and is received inked per the pathology \u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; \u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A radiograph \u000areceived with the specimen reveals that a wire and dense tissue is present \u000awithin the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a focus of \u000acircumscribed, soft tan/yellow tissue (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.4cm ) that is located REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the superior and lateral margins, 0.3cm from the medial, 0.7cm from \u000athe anterior, and distant from the skin. There is a focus of tan/gray material \u000a(0.4cm in greatest dimension) abutting the mass on its posterior aspect and \u000alocated less than 0.1cm from the superior margin. An additional firm, \u000acircumscribed nodule (0.3cm in greatest diameter) is located 0.9cm from the \u000amain tissue density, 0.2cm from the medial, 0.4cm from the lateral, 0.6cm \u000afrom the anterior, 1.1cm from the superior margin, 1.5cm from the inferior \u000amargin, and 1.0cm from the skin, and distant from the posterior margin. The \u000aremainder of the specimen consists of 10% firm white fibrous tissue and 90% \u000ayellow fatty tissue. The specimen is entirely submitted \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : firm nodule and adjacent tissue, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID a6: tissue between firm nodule and additional ? lesional tissue, \u000a1 frag each cassette, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : ? lesional tissue, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : tan/gray nodule and adjacent tissue, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : remainder of breast tissue, multiple frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : skin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aLYS/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
