, black/posterior aspect, \u000aorange/lateral aspect, and yellow/medial aspect. The specimen is sectioned \u000afrom the inferior to superior aspect to reveal adipose tissue and firm fibrous \u000atissue (4.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 2.5cm ), comprising the medial, anterior, and posterior \u000amargins of the specimen. The specimen is entirely submitted for histologic \u000aevaluation. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : most inferior section, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice two from inferior, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice three, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice four, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice five, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice six, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice seven, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : most superior slice eight, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMRU/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Marianna B Ruzinova M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
