VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Kristin S. Ricci, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Gilbert Brodsky, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. LEFT BREAST, CENTRAL TISSUE: \u000aBenign breast tissue with scattered microcysts. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the \u000aspecimen, including margins. \u000aNo atypical ductal hyperplasia present. \u000aNOTE: \u000aMicrocalcifications are not identified on examination of entire specimen \u000ahistologically. Radiographs were taken of the block, revealing no definite \u000aareas of calcification. Multiple levels were examined of several blocks with \u000aquestionable radiographic foci of calcifications. \u000aB. LEFT BREAST LATERAL TISSUE: \u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, clinging, solid, and cribriform patterns. \u000aMargins are free of atypical ductal hyperplasia. \u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium. \u000aCore biopsy site. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 50 year old woman, S/P REMOVED_DATE L breast core bxs revealing ADH - has \u000aseparate area of suspicious calcs and be excised also. \u000aOperation: L breast WL bx (2 wires). \u000aOperative Findings: Not provided. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) L breast central tissue - short/superior, long/lateral, whip/deep margin \u000a(to mammo). \u000aB/2) L breast lateral tissue - short/superior, long/lateral, whip stitch/chest \u000awall margin (to mammo). \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aSpecimen #1: Few faint calcifications seen near the specimen margins - wire \u000aseparate from specimen. Discussed with Dr. Rhei by Dr. S. Raza @ 10:55 am. \u000aSpecimen #2: Report called to Dr. Rhei by Dr. Gaber @ 11:22 - specimen mass \u000atargeted bx clip and wire. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname, unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "#1. L breast central tissue - short suture superior long - \u000alateral whipstitch - deep tissue", consists of a partial mastectomy specimen, \u000awhich is 4.5cm superior to inferior, 3.5cm medial to lateral, and 1.2cm \u000aanterior to posterior. The specimen is received with a long suture designating \u000alateral and a short suture designating superior. The specimen is received \u000ainked by the surgeon for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. An \u000aaccompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates an unattached localizing wire and \u000athe partial mastectomy specimen with areas of focal calcifications. The \u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior and submitted \u000aentirely. The breast parenchyma consists of 80% fibrous tissue and 20% adipose \u000atissue. No masses are identified grossly. The specimen is submitted entirely. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior resection margin, radially sectioned, multi frags each, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections of the specimen from superior to inferior, multi \u000afrags each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Inferior resection margin, radially sectioned, 4 frags each, ESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "#2. L breast lateral tissue short - superior long - lateral \u000awhipstitch chest wall margin", consists of a wire localized partial mastectomy \u000aspecimen (4.3cm anterior to posterior, 2.7cm inferior to superior, and 1.5cm \u000amedial to lateral). The specimen is received inked by the surgeon for the \u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A short suture is used to \u000adesignated superior, a long suture is designated lateral and a whipstitch \u000adesignates deep. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a localizing \u000awire entering the anterior aspect of the specimen and indicating a surgical \u000aclip and fibrous tissue. The specimen is serially sectioned from anterior to \u000aposterior and submitted entirely. The specimen consists of 85% fibrous tissue \u000aand 15% adipose tissue. The biopsy clip is 0.9cm from the inferior resection \u000amargin, 0.4cm from the lateral resection margin, 0.5cm from the medial \u000aresection margin, and 1.6cm from the superior resection margin. The specimen \u000ais greater than 1.0cm from the anterior and posterior resection margins. \u000aPlease note: the specimen is received with bled ink and margins are best \u000areapproximated with re-inking. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Anterior resection margin, radially sectioned, 6 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections of the specimen, with biopsy clip in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , 1 frag \u000aeach, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Posterior resection margin, radially sectioned, multi frags each, \u000aESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aRIC/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday \u000aREMOVED_MONTH 02,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 04:50:06PM \u000a[report_end] 
