VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: GREGORY J KANE M.D. \u000aCASE: BG-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Matthias D. Hofer, M.D. \u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP MARKING A \u000aPRIOR CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY SITE: \u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA with apocrine features and marked nuclear \u000aatypia, is present involving an intraductal papilloma and in adjacent tissue in \u000a2 of 7 of blocks (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; levels examined) (see note). Single cell \u000anecrosis is present in one duct measuring \u000a< 0.1cm (present in deeper levels of block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aNumerous calcifications are present associated with the papilloma and focally \u000ain adjacent breast tissue. The calcifications do not appear to be specifically \u000aassociated with the areas of atypia. \u000aThe core needle biopsy site involves the papilloma. \u000aNote: There is a cribriform proliferation of cells, partially involving the \u000apapilloma, that has apocrine features and focally marked nuclear pleomorphism \u000ain block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . The involved areas all measure < 0.2cm in size. In block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , \u000athere is a < 0.1cm area of cells with marked nuclear pleomorphism involving a \u000alobular unit. Although these areas approach a diagnosis of DCIS, given the \u000asmall size of the involved spaces and the involvement of a papilloma, a \u000adiagnosis of ADH is favored. The ADH in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  is 0.1cm from the junction \u000aof inferior and deep margins, which is the closest margin. The ADH in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000ais 0.3cm from the closest deep margin. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed, but the areas with the marked nuclear \u000aatypia are not present on the deeper levels. Areas of associated epithelial \u000aproliferation in the papilloma are estrogen and progesterone receptor positive \u000aand show only weak partial membrane immunoreactivity (considered a negative \u000aresult). \u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester and Dr. Deborah Dillon, who \u000aconcur with this interpretation. \u000aRIGHT BREAST, DESIGNATED ?TISSUE LATERAL TO SPECIMEN #1?: \u000aCysts. \u000aNote: ADH is not seen in this specimen. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 75-year-old with right breast microcalcifications on mammogram. ADH \u000aon core biopsy (Faulkner). \u000aOperation: Right breast lumpectomy. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Atypical ductal hyperplasia right breast. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Right breast tissue. \u000aB/2. Breast tissue lateral to specimen #1, unoriented. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Calcifications and clip in specimen, no wire. \u000aReport called to Dr. Kane on REMOVED_DATE at 12:20 PM. Brad Talley, M.D., Radiology \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in 2 parts, each labeled with the patient?s \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right breast tissue with wire, short inferior aspect, long \u000amedial aspect" was received accompanied by a radiograph without wire that shows \u000ascattered microcalcifications and a clip. The specimen came with two stitches \u000aattached, which per surgeon, indicate the inferior aspect (short stitch) and \u000athe long stitch indicating the medial aspect. The specimen is inked per \u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. \u000aThe specimen measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.4cm (S-I, A-P, M-L). The specimen was \u000aserially sectioned from medial to lateral in seven sections and revealed a REMOVED_DATE \u000ax 0.3cm hemorrhagic well-circumscribed round area in slices #1 and #2 (lateral \u000aend). There is a REMOVED_DATE x 0.4cm brown/yellow discoloration abutting the anterior \u000amargin of the specimen. This area is more visible in slices #3 and #4. \u000aMultiple microscopic calcifications were present. The clip was retrieved in \u000aslice 4. The specimen was entirely submitted for histological review. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #1, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #4, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #5, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #6, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #7, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aPart B, "#2. Breast tissue lateral to specimen #1-unoriented", consists of two \u000afibroadipose tissue fragments (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.7cm and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.7cm ), \u000awhich are bisected and entirely submitted for histological review. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Additional tissue, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER: BG-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000aMH/am \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
