VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aPathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS \u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D. \u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D. \u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A CLIP AND CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aSeverely atypical ductal hyperplasia (<0.2cm ) within 0.1cm of the \u000amargin (block 6; levels examined) (see NOTE). \u000aAdditional areas of less severe atypical ductal hyperplasia are also \u000apresent. \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid extension into ducts is \u000apresent, associated with the prior needle biopsy site (e.g. blocks 2 & 3) \u000aand away from the biopsy site (e.g. block 16). \u000aIntraductal papillomas. \u000aFibroadenoma, 0.3cm  \u000aFlorid epithelial hyperplasia. \u000aSclerosing adenosis. \u000aNumerous apocrine cysts with calcifications. \u000aNOTE: The area of atypical ductal hyperplasia has micropapillary \u000afeatures. Differential diagnosis would include a small focus of low grade \u000aductal carcinoma in situ. However, given the small size of the involved \u000aarea, a diagnosis of severely atypical ductal hyperplasia is favored. \u000aThis slide was also reviewed by Dr. James Connolly. \u000aThe prior core biopsy is reviewed ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). In conjunction with the \u000afindings on this excision, the core biopsy is best interpreted as lobular \u000acarcinoma in situ. \u000aThe calcifications present appear to be associated with cysts and \u000aepithelium and not specifically with the areas of atypical hyperplasia. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Right abnormal mammogram. Positive core, ? LCIS on REMOVED_DATE  \u000aOperation: Needle-directed breast biopsy. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1 Right breast mass to mammo and for permanent. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by CHAN,JENNIFER A.,M.D.,C.M. \u000aReceived fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "#1 Right \u000abreast mass", is a single, unoriented fragment of yellow/tan fibroadipose \u000atissue (8.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 3.0cm ) with a needle localization wire in place. The \u000aspecimen is mostly adipose tissue (80% fat, 20% breast parenchyma). There is a \u000a3.0 x 0.1cm pink/tan hemorrhagic biopsy tract identified, approximately 0.3cm \u000afrom the needle-localization wire. There are no nodules or masses suspicious \u000afor invasive carcinoma. All fibrous tissue and breast parenchyma are submitted \u000ain twenty (20) cassettes. The remaining tissue consists exclusively of adipose \u000atissue. The specimen is radiographed, and the staple is localized in block \u000a#2. There are calcifications in blocks #6 and #12. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Right breast mass, multiple fragments, RSS. \u000ajch \u000aReports to: SHOJI, BRENT, T. M.D. \u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS \u000a[report_end] 
