teral (long suture) margins. The specimen is \u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange \u000a(inked by prosector). Sectioning demonstrates at least 95% tan/white lobulated \u000afibrodense tissue. A discrete mass is not seen. The entire specimen is \u000asubmitted, sequentially from lateral to medial, with perpendicular lateral \u000amargin in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and perpendicular medial margin in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, "#2. Abdominal wall mass", consists of an aggregate of fragmented at \u000aleast partially encapsulated soft, lobulated adipose tissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm). Please note: True surgical margins cannot be assessed due to the \u000adisrupted, fragmented nature of the specimen. Cut surfaces are uniformly \u000atan/yellow and unremarkable, lacking hemorrhage, necrosis or calcification \u000agrossly. Representative sections (one per centimeter in greatest dimension) \u000aare submitted for routine processing. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Multi frags, RSS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000aDB/ajb \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jeffrey F Krane M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a11/15/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
