men, the internal tissue is approximately 50% fibrous and 50% adipose. No \u000adiscrete masses or lumps were identified. There was one small area of \u000ahemorrhage at the end of one of the localization wires. A prior biopsy site \u000awas near (0.3cm ) the superior margin. Approximately 80% of the specimen was \u000asubmitted for histological analysis. This included approximately 85% to 90% of \u000athe fibrous tissue and the entire area within the wires that contain the \u000acalcifications. Approximately 95% of the lateral margin and 75% of the medial \u000amargin were submitted for histologic analysis. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin, perpendicular, 2 frags and 1 frag respectively, \u000aRSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Previous biopsy site, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Area between the localization wires, with  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  areas of \u000atermination of the wires, multi frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Additional fibrous areas of breast tissue, 1 frag each, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin, perpendicular, 2 frags each, RSS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000atac/elw \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 30,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05:13:43PM \u000a[report_end] 
