y-o with R abn/mammo REMOVED_DATE 25-cm of calcs R UOQ. \u000aCore bx REMOVED_DATE --> atypical lobular hyperplasia, ADH. \u000aPrior R breast bx REMOVED_DATE --> ADH. \u000aOperation: R breast wire loc bx. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right abnormal mammogram. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Biopsy, clip and calcification clusters contained in specimen \u000a(clipped marked site of ADH biopsy. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000a1. Right breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral, whip \u000astitch deep margin. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit number \u000aand "1. Right breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral, whip \u000astitch deep margin". It consists of a previously inked breast excision \u000aspecimen (5.1 from medial to lateral, x REMOVED_DATE from superior to inferior, x 1.4cm \u000afrom anterior to posterior), containing a long and a short stitch designated as \u000alateral and superior, respectively. No whip stitch is identified. Within the \u000aspecimen is a single localization wire, which penetrates the specimen through \u000athe lateral margin and exits through the posterior margin. The accompanying \u000aspecimen radiograph shows multiple microcalcifications, adjacent to which are \u000athe localization wire and a single clip. Within the specimen is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x \u000a0.2cm white-grey fibrotic area, corresponding to where the wire was placed. It \u000agrossly abuts the lateral margin, and is 0.5cm from the posterior, 0.7cm from \u000athe superior, 0.9cm from the inferior, and 0.9cm from the medial margin. No \u000ablue ink (anterior margin) is identified in the specimen. No discrete masses \u000aare grossly seen. The specimen is serially sectioned and submitted from \u000alateral to medial. The specimen is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial sections from lateral to medial, Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  containing the \u000afibrotic area with microcalcifications, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000apal/MMM \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Paula C Abreu-e-Lima M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Paula C Abreu-e-Lima M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Shuji Ogino M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a12/31/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aFIRST ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE Dr. Deborah Dillon \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for in situ carcinoma (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) POSITIVE (2+) \u000aInterpretation: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor \u000acells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but \u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive. \u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane \u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity \u000ain >10% of tumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these