VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-08- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Cheryl Adackapara, M.D.; Eleanor Y. Chen, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (best seen in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aCore needle biopsy site changes. \u000aEpithelial hyperplasia. \u000aColumnar cell changes. \u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium. \u000aApocrine cysts. \u000aNote: The patient had a prior core needle biopsy in the central outer \u000abreast showing atypical ductal hyperplasia ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). Clinical and \u000aradiologic correlation is needed to determine if this specimen is an excision \u000aof this site. \u000aThere is no malignancy or atypical ductal hyperplasia seen upon \u000amicroscopic examination of the entire specimen, including margins. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 58 year old woman with recent abnormal mammogram which showed an area \u000aof microcalcifications in the lower inner quadrant of the right breast. A \u000abiopsy of this area showed columnar cell change with calcifications. However, \u000aa core needle biopsy of the central outer breast showed ADH. \u000aOperation: None given. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right abnormal mammogram. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Right breast tissue. \u000aRADIOLOGY: Tissue marker and faint residual ca++ in specimen. Hookwire also \u000apresent. C. Denison, MD. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name, \u000aunit number, and "R breast tissue", and consists of a fragment of fibroadipose \u000atissue (5.2 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ) with a short suture designating superior and a long \u000asuture designating lateral. The specimen is received with a \u000aneedle-localization wire entering through the posterolateral aspect and exiting \u000athrough the posteromedial aspect as well as a specimen radiograph designating a \u000ametallic clip within the specimen adjacent to the needle-localization wire. A \u000asingle calcification is seen adjacent to the clip. The specimen is inked by \u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/ orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from anterior to posterior \u000ato exhibit a red-brown hemorrhagic area (0.6 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm ) consistent with a \u000aprevious biopsy site which also contains the metallic clip. This area is \u000alocated abutting the inferior margin, 0.4cm from the superior margin, 1.1cm \u000afrom the medial margin, 1.3cm from the lateral margin, 0.8cm from the \u000aposterior margin, and 3.7cm from the anterior margin. The remaining breast \u000aparenchyma consists of approximately 70% dense white focally cystic fibrous \u000atissue to 30% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen is sequentially \u000aentirely submitted from posterior to anterior. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular posterior margin, multi frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Representative sections submitted from anterior to include area \u000aof biopsy cavity ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) and slice containing metallic clip ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), multi frags \u000atotal, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular anterior margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aJC/ag \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Monday \u000aREMOVED_MONTH 22,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05:16:02PM \u000a[report_end] 
