VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Stacey A. Simons, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR ARCHITECTURAL DISTORTION WITH \u000aCALCIFICATIONS: \u000aComplex sclerosing lesion with sclerosing adenosis, intraductal papillomas, \u000acalcifications, and epithelial hyperplasia, 1.5cm (see note). \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia, small focus (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aColumnar cell change with flat atypia (e.g. block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aNo biopsy site changes are seen. \u000aNote: The patient is s/p core needle biopsies for this lesion that showed \u000anormal breast tissue (see  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). The clip and the biopsy site are not \u000apresent in the specimen, which may indicate that the prior biopsy did not \u000asample this lesion. \u000aThe specimen radiograph shows an area of density that correlates with the \u000acomplex sclerosing lesion. Clinical/radiologic correlation are suggested to \u000aensure that the original radiologic lesion was excised in this specimen. \u000aNo carcinoma is seen after complete microscopic evaluation of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 43-year-old premenopausal female with mammographic architectural \u000adistortion with calcifications. Stereotactic core biopsy showed normal breast \u000atissue. Breast MRI showed enhancement of site. Mammography recommended \u000aexcision for radiologic-pathologic discordance. \u000aOperation: L breast wire-localized biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: No interpretation of the radiology report is provided. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, short suture/superior, long suture/lateral, \u000awhipstitch on deep margin. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number, \u000aand "left breast", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.5cm excision of \u000afibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating superior (short suture), \u000alateral (long suture), and deep margin (whipstitch). The specimen is inked per \u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange \u000a(inked by surgeon). The localizing wire enters the specimen inferomedially and \u000aexits posteriorly. The accompanying radiograph shows a localizing wire in \u000aplace within the specimen adjacent to an encircled area of an ill-defined dense \u000amass. Sectioning demonstrates tan/white focally granular fibrous tissue (at \u000aleast 40% of the specimen), with an area of hemorrhage associated with the \u000alocalizing wire tract. Fibrous tissue in the area of interest shows focal \u000adilated ducts filled with a tan/yellow, thick fluid (at least 2.5cm in \u000agreatest dimension) abutting multiple margins, particularly inferior and \u000aposterior. Associated fibrous tissue radiates to all margins. There are no \u000adefinitive masses. Entire specimen is submitted, sequentially from inferior to \u000asuperior, with area of interest as marked by wire in micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Entire specimen, multiple frags, ESS (Note: Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = \u000ainferior; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = inferior, lateral; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = \u000alateral, medial, inferior; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = posterior, lateral, \u000amedial; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = inferior, medial, lateral; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = \u000aposterior, medial, lateral; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = anterior, lateral; \u000amicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = posterior, medial, lateral; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = lateral, \u000aanterior, medial; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = medial; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = posterior, \u000alateral, medial; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = medial, posterior;  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = \u000aposterior, lateral, superior;  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = superior, medial; micro \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = superior, medial, posterior; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = medial, \u000asuperior; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = medial, superior, posterior; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a= medial; micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  = superior. Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  are \u000ainferior and superior margins, perpendicular sections, \u000arespectively). \u000adb/lp \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a10/6/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
