VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Wai Chin Foo, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST, 6:00, MRI GUIDED WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR TWO ADJACENT NODULES, \u000a0.6 CM AND REMOVED_DATE CM: \u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, is present in a pagetoid pattern, in two \u000afibroadenomatoid nodules, REMOVED_DATE and 0.4cm (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) and in one duct \u000aaway from the nodules (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) (see note). One of the nodules is present at \u000athe medial margin. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for ADH \u000a(block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and e-cadherin on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu NEGATIVE (1+) \u000aE-cadherin POSITIVE \u000aNote: The two nodules involved by ADH correlate with the MRI findings. The \u000aductal spaces are lined by cells with markedly pleomorphic nuclei and frequent \u000amitoses with occasional atypical forms. However, there are no architectural \u000achanges such as cribriform spaces or micropapillae. Therefore, classification \u000aas ADH is favored. Selected slides were also reviewed by Dr. Andrea \u000aRichardson, Dr. Deborah Dillon, and Dr. Stuart Schnitt, who concur with this \u000ainterpretation. \u000aThe entire specimen was examined microscopically, including margins. \u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER INNER, MRI GUIDED WIRE LOCALIZED EXCIISON FOR A REMOVED_DATE CM OVAL \u000aENHANCING MASS: \u000aColumnar cell change with flat atypia, involving a complex sclerosing lesion \u000a(approximately 1cm ), best seen in sides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  (see note). \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, low nuclear grade, with a cribriform pattern (best \u000aseen in blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aAtypical hyperplasia, in a pagetoid pattern (best seen in blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for ADH and \u000acolumnar cell change (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu NEGATIVE (0 to 1+) \u000aE-cadherin POSITIVE \u000aNote: The complex sclerosing lesion may correlate with the MRI enhancing \u000alesion. The entire specimen was examined microscopically, including margins. \u000aSelected slides were also reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson, Dr. Deborah \u000aDillon, and Dr. Stuart Schnitt, who concur with this interpretation. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Abnormal L breast mass/MRI. \u000aOperation: L partial mastectomy. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal MRI L breast. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Left breast 6 o'clock breast tissue short - superior, long lateral, \u000awhipped stitch - deep margin. \u000aB/1. Left upper, inner quad tissue short - superior, long - lateral, whipped \u000astitch deep margin. \u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Intact hookwire and tissue are within the specimens. MRI \u000aguided localizations. Please submit entire tissue Dr. Gombas. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled ?Left breast 6 o'clock breast tissue short - superior, long \u000alateral, whipped stitch - deep margin?, consists of a wire localized breast \u000aexcision (4.2cm from anterior to superior, 3.0cm from superior to inferior, \u000aand 1.5cm from medial to lateral). A localization wire is in place. A short \u000asuture is marking the superior margin, a long suture the lateral margin. A \u000awhipped stitch at the posterior margin is not present. An accompanying \u000aspecimen radiograph demonstrates the localization wire, and some ill-defined \u000aradiodense areas in the periphery of the specimen. The specimen has been \u000apreviously inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of \u000amargins: posterior = black, anterior = blue, superior = red, inferior = green, \u000alateral = yellow, medial = orange. Ink is reapplied. The specimen is cut into \u000a12 slices from anterior to posterior. The breast tissue contains dense fibrous \u000aparenchyma throughout the whole specimen. A distinct grey round area is \u000aidentified in slices 6 and 7 (0.5cm ), abutting the posterior margin. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Anterior margin, perpendicular, 4 frags each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ? REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #2-#11, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Posterior margin, perpendicular, REMOVED_DATE frags, ESS \u000aPart B, labeled ?Left upper, inner quad. tissue short - superior, long - \u000alateral, whipped stitch deep margin?, consists of a wire localized breast \u000aexcision (6.1cm from medial to lateral, 3.2cm from superior to inferior, and \u000a3.0cm from anterior to posterior). A localization wire is in place. A short \u000asuture is marking the superior margin, a long suture the lateral margin, and a \u000awhipped stitch the deep margin. An accompanying specimen radiograph \u000ademonstrates the localization wire, and ill-defined radiodense areas throughout \u000athe specimen. The specimen has been previously inked by the surgeon per \u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black, anterior = \u000ablue, superior = red, inferior = green, lateral = yellow, medial = orange. Ink \u000ais reapplied. The specimen is cut into 19 slices from medial to lateral. The \u000abreast tissue contains dense white fibrous areas extending from the medial to \u000athe lateral margin. Small cysts (0.1cm ), filled with yellow material, are \u000aidentified in slices 11 and 12, 0.2cm from the posterior/inferior margin. 2 \u000acysts (up to 0.4cm ) are present in slice 18 at the lateral margin. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin, perpendicular, 5 frags, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #3, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #4, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #5, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #6, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #7, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #8, REMOVED_DATE frags, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #9, REMOVED_DATE frags, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #10, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #11, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #12, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #13, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #14, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #15, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #16, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #17, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #18, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Slice #19, multi frags each, ESS \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a10/11/ REMOV