u000aentering the superior medial aspect of the specimen with no definitive exit. \u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit 20% dense \u000awhite fibrous tissue to 80% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The fibrous \u000atissue is located along the access of the needle localization wire. No masses \u000aor lesions are identified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular medial margin, 3 frag in each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Body, sequentially submitted from medial to lateral to include \u000afibrous area adjacent to needle localization wire ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), multi frags total, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "right breast middle tissue axillary site", consists of an \u000airregular fragment of fibroadipose tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.5cm ) with a stitch \u000adesignating lateral per surgeon. The lateral aspect is inked blue and the \u000aremaining surface is black. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit 70% \u000adense white fibrous tissues with 30% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The \u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Multi frags total, ESS. \u000aJenC/mmm \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on Monday \u000aREMOVED_MONTH 19,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 10:40:37AM \u000a[report_end] 
