VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aPathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS \u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aKRANE, JEFFREY, FRED, M.D.~PH.D. \u000aDIAGNOSIS: by KRANE,JEFFREY FRED,M.D.,PH.D. \u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZATION OF A PRIOR CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY SITE: \u000aFocal epithelial hyperplasia. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aNote: The patient had a prior core needle biopsy for calcifications which \u000arevealed atypical ductal hyperplasia. A clip was not placed. \u000aThe original calcifications were removed by the core needle biopsy. The \u000aradiologist localized an area consistent with a hematoma for this \u000aexcision. In the specimen radiograph, the radiologist noted two benign \u000aappearing calcifications, not definitively associated with the biopsy \u000asite. \u000aThe specimen radiograph reveals very faint possible calcifications and a \u000asmall density. The entire specimen is submitted for histologic \u000aexamination, and multiple levels are examined, including margins. No \u000adefinite calcifications are identified. The blocks are radiographed and a \u000apossible small calcification is located in block 3. Multiple levels do \u000anot reveal histologic evidence of calcification. \u000aA biopsy site or hematoma consistent with the prior core needle biopsy \u000asite are not identified. Clinical and radiologic correlation is suggested \u000ato ensure that this excision includes the site of the prior core needle \u000abiopsy. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: A 65 year old postmenopausal female, on hormone replacement therapy \u000awhose recent mammogram demonstrated a 5mm cluster of calcifications \u000ain the left breast. Core biopsy revealed atypical ductal hyperplasia. \u000aOperation: Left breast wire localization excision \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast ADH \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1) Left breast mass \u000aRADIOLOGY: Two benign appearing calcifications in specimen, not definitely \u000aassociated with the biopsy site. J. Leung, MD \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by OH,KIRSTINE YUNG-TING,M.D. \u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name, \u000aunit number and "left breast mass inking per pathology protocol". Attached \u000awith the specimen is a mammogram demonstrating a fragment of breast tissue with \u000aa wire localizing needle in the specimen, a small density, and very faint \u000acalcifications. The specimen measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm and is received inked \u000aper pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; \u000aanterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = \u000aorange. Serial sectioning reveals yellow/tan fatty tissue throughout the \u000aspecimen. No focal lesions or fibrous tissue is identified. The specimen is \u000aentirely submitted from medial to lateral. \u000aMicro 1: medial border of breast tissue, 2 frags, ESS \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  serial sections of breast tissue, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro 11: lateral border of breast tissue, 2 frags, ESS \u000ako \u000aReports to: KAELIN, CAROLYN, MARY M.D. \u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS \u000a[report_end] 
