osis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) WEAKLY POSITIVE (2+) \u000aNote: FISH analysis for amplification of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu may be helpful, if \u000aclinically warranted. \u000aInterpretation: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a \u000aportion of the membrane is positive. \u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of \u000atumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% \u000aof tumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational \u000ause only. \u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (8/17/05): \u000aFISH was performed by USLABS using the PathVysion HER-2 DNA Probe Kit with the \u000afollowing results (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aResult: NOT AMPLIFIED \u000aThe Her-2/cen 1