VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANNE GEORGIAN-SMITH M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Jane E Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, NEAR PRIOR EXCISION SITE, 8 G STEREOTACTIC CORE \u000aNEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson \u000agrade III/III), with apocrine features, measuring at least 0.7cm in size (see \u000anote). \u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen in this limited specimen. \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, comedo and solid types (high nuclear grade) with \u000acalcifications and necrosis, is present. \u000aNOTE: By report, the patient has a history of invasive carcinoma in  REMOVED_CASE_ID . No \u000afurther information about this carcinoma is available. Comparison to the prior \u000acarcinoma may be of value to determine if this is more likely a recurrence or a \u000anew primary carcinoma. \u000aImmunohistochemical analysis for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu will be performed and reported in an addendum. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHISTORY: History of right breast cancer treated with radiation therapy 6 years \u000aago. New linear Ca++ UOQ near bx scar right beast. Calcifications highly \u000asuspicious for DCIS. \u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING: Calcifications \u000aLOCATION: Right: upper outer \u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY: 8-G Stereotactic Vacuum-assisted \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) 4 cores, yellow cassette, calcifications in all cores. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit \u000anumber, and "right breast", and consists of one yellow cassette labeled "A \u000aCA++", containing multiple yellow/tan to tan/pink soft tissues (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm in aggregate). \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Yellow cassette content (A Ca++), multi frags, ESS. \u000alm/lp \u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07- REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a7/2/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) WEAKLY POSITIVE (EQUIVOCAL) (2+) \u000aNote: FISH analysis for amplification of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu may be helpful, if \u000aclinically warranted. \u000aCorrelation with the immunoprofile of the patient's prior breast carcinoma is \u000asuggested. \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a \u000aportion of the membrane is positive. \u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of \u000atumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of \u000atumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational \u000ause only. \u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
