VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: Esther Rhei M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Briana Gleason, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, RE-EXCISION: \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, micropapillary, solid and cribriform (intermediate \u000anuclear grade), with associated necrosis and calcification, is present in 5 of \u000a20 blocks (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aDCIS is present less than 0.1cm from medial margin ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 0.1cm from the \u000asuperior ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) and lateral ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) margins and 0.15cm from the medial margin \u000a( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with features of DCIS is present less than 0.1cm \u000afrom the medial margin ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aSkeletal muscle is present and shows no involvement by carcinoma. \u000aCalcifications are present in association with benign epithelium. \u000aBiopsy site changes. \u000aNOTE: Immunoperoxidase study for E-Cadherin support the above diagnosis. \u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, Breast Pathology Service, who concurs. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 46 y.o. premenopausal female with hx of L breast ADH, now with R \u000aabnormal mammo. R breast wire loc excision on REMOVED_DATE -> severe ADH bordering \u000aon DCIS with close superior deep margins \u000aOperation: None given \u000aOperative Findings: None given \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000a1. Right breast mass, long lateral suture, short superior suture, skin anterior \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number, \u000aand "#1 right breast mass", and consists of a 28-gram ovoid breast excision \u000a(5.8cm S-I x 3.8cm M-L x 2.2cm A-P) oriented by the surgeon (short suture= \u000asuperior, long=lateral). The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the \u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is received with a \u000acentral full-thickness defect (2.1 x 2.0cm ), which extends through the \u000aanterior and posterior margins. Definitive skin is not identified on the \u000aanterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from \u000asuperior to inferior into 12 slices (slice 1 equals most superior). In slices \u000a3-7, there is a well-delineated, red-brown to tan-white fibrous, partially \u000afragmented area (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ) grossly consistent with a biopsy cavity, \u000awhich is 0.1cm from the posterior margin, 0.2cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the medial margin, 0.4cm from the lateral margin, 0.4cm from the \u000asuperior margin, and 1.2cm from the inferior margin. Adjacent to the presumed \u000acavity is abundant chalky gritty yellow-tan fat necrosis. The remainder of the \u000aspecimen is composed of approximately dense, focally cystic (0.1-0.4cm ) \u000afibrous stroma extending to all surgical margins and encompassing the \u000aaforementioned biopsy cavity, and approximately 20% yellow-tan lobulated \u000aadipose tissue. No additional discrete lesions are noted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 3, bisected to include biopsy cavity with closest superior \u000amargin, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 4, bisected to include biopsy cavity with closest posterior, \u000alateral, anterior and medial margin, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 5, bisected biopsy cavity with adjacent fat necrosis and \u000aadditional margins, 1 frag each, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 6, sectioned biopsy cavity with adjacent fat necrosis and \u000adense stroma, 3 frags total, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 7, bisected remaining biopsy cavity with adjacent dense \u000astroma and additional margins, 1 frag each, ESS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 9, dense focally cystic stroma with anterior, medial, inferior \u000a(closest to biopsy cavity), 1 frag, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 10, dense/cystic stroma with anterior, inferior, and lateral \u000amargins, 1 frag, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : slice 11, dense/cystic stroma with anterior and inferior margins, 1 \u000afrag, RSS \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : remainder of specimen, mult frags, ESS \u000aLMB/jp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Briana Gleason M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on \u000a5/20/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the \u000afollowing results for DCIS (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu (c-erb- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) WEAKLY POSITIVE - HETEROGENEOUS \u000aInterpretation: \u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the \u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows: \u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive \u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive \u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet \u000abeen determined. \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu analysis: \u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells. \u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a \u000aportion of the membrane is positive. \u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of \u000atumor cells. \u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% \u000aof tumor cells. \u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result. \u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational \u000ause only. \u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
