ology per pathology protocol for \u000athe evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red; \u000ainferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. Three short sutures (not \u000aused for orientation) are present holding together a transected part of the \u000aspecimen along the inferior margin. The specimen is sectioned at 3mm \u000aintervals from lateral to medial revealing 90% of the specimen to be composed \u000aof rubbery white fibrous tissue, without firm foci suggestive of invasive \u000acarcinoma, and the remaining 10% being adipose tissue. The fibrous tissue \u000aextends to all but the superior and lateral margins. The specimen is entirely \u000asubmitted. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 11 frags, ESS \u000aPart two, labeled "#2", consists of a portion of fibroadipose tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax 0.9cm ) which is received in pathology already inked per pathology protocol \u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = \u000ared; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The specimen is also \u000areceived with a specimen radiograph which is reported as clip in specimen. The \u000aspecimen is serially sectioned at 3mm intervals from lateral to medial \u000arevealing a specimen which is completely composed of soft rubbery white fibrous \u000atissue with no firm areas to suggest invasive carcinoma. The clip is \u000aidentified within the specimen near the inferior margin (submitted in cassette \u000a8). The specimen is entirely submitted. \u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 11 frags, ESS \u000aeab \u000aReports to: LESNIKOSKI, BETH-ANN M.D. \u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS \u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SURGICAL MARGIN \u000a[report_end] 
