le localization wire in placed entering \u000ainferolateral aspect of the specimen and exiting the posteromedial aspect of \u000athe specimen. The specimen was previously radiographed and review of the \u000aaccompanying radiograph shows the presence of calcification and an intact \u000ahookwire within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral \u000ato medial. The cut surface is composed of approximately 40% dense/nodular \u000apink/tan focally cystic fibrous stroma (particularly in the central/medial \u000aaspect of the specimen), and approximately 60% yellow/tan adipose tissue. The \u000aneedle localization wire traverses the dense fibrous stroma in the central \u000aaspect of the specimen, corresponding to the calcifications on radiograph. No \u000adiscrete masses or lesions are grossly identified. The specimen is entirely \u000asequentially submitted from lateral to medial. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags in each, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Central specimen sequentially submitted from lateral to medial, \u000awith area of calcifications in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , multi frags total, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags total, ESS. \u000alm/ag \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on \u000a7/5/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
