VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Stefan Kraft, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present (best seen in blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (best seen in blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). An \u000aimmunoperoxidase study for e-cadherin demonstrates positivity in an area of ADH \u000a(block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). These areas of ADH are not specifically associated with \u000acalcifications. \u000aColumnar cell change with atypia is associated with calcifications. \u000aNote: No invasive carcinoma or DCIS are seen after complete microscopic \u000aexamination of the specimen, including margins. \u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Deborah Dillon, who concurs with this \u000ainterpretation. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Abnormal R mammo, increase in calcifications. \u000aOperation: Right needle-directed breast biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: Not provided. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast mass. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) R breast tissue, stitch - short/superior, long/lateral, to mammo  REMOVED_CASE_ID  pm. \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aCalcifications and intact wire in specimen, reported to Dr. Shoji @ 1:30 p.m. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, \u000aand "R breast tissue, stitch, short equals superior, long equals lateral", and \u000aconsists of a tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose soft tissue (4.5 x \u000a3.5 x 1.0cm ). There is a loosely attached needle localization wire in place \u000aentering through the superior/anterior aspect of the specimen. There is a \u000ashort suture designating the superior margin and a long suture designating the \u000alateral margin. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol (superior/red, \u000ainferior/green, lateral/yellow, medial/orange, anterior/blue, and \u000aposterior/black). The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior \u000aand the cut surface displays focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue, which \u000aconstitutes approximately 55% of the breast parenchyma. The remainder of the \u000abreast parenchyma is tan/yellow and homogenous with no areas of disruption or \u000alesions identified. The specimen is received with a radiograph, which \u000ademonstrates an area of calcifications located within the inferior medial \u000aaspect of the specimen. The specimen is submitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Sequential cross sections, superior to inferior, 1 frag \u000aeach, ESS with possible area of calcification with en bloc, \u000a12-15. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Inferior margin, 1 frag, ESS. \u000asek/lp \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a9/6/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
