VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Edilberto G Bauza, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Jonathan N Glickman, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA: SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST AREOLAR NEEDLE LOCALIZATION BIOPSY": \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, columnar hyperplasia, adenosis, and \u000aintraductal papilloma. \u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium. \u000a(Levels examined.) \u000aB: SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST UPPER/OUTER LOCALIZED WIRE BIOPSY" \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, columnar cell hyperplasia, adenosis. \u000aCyst with focal chronic inflammation and necrosis, giant cell reaction. \u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium and artery. \u000aAtypical hyperplasia focally involves adenosis. \u000aCore biopsy site. \u000aImmunostain for p63 highlights myoepithelial cells in one focus of adenosis, \u000asupporting the above diagnosis. \u000a(Levels examined.) \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Status-post right core biopsy REMOVED_DATE  outside path consult REMOVED_DATE - \u000aatypical lobular and ductal hyperplasia. \u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized biopsies  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1 - right breast areolar wire localized biopsy, short suture->superior, long \u000asuture->lateral (to mammo). \u000aB/2 - rt breast upper/outer localized wire biopsy  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , short suture->superior, \u000along suture->lateral (to mammo). \u000aRADIOGRAPHY REPORT: #1 Calcifications and wire in specimen. Elman, M.D. \u000a#2 Clip, wire and calcs in specimen. Elman, M.D. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "#1 - right breast areolar needle localized biopsy", is \u000areceived with a radiograph and a single localizing wire in place, and consists \u000aof a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.3cm firm fibrofatty tissue fragment, oriented by sutures \u000adesignating superior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins, which \u000aare inked by the pro-sector as follows per the pathology protocol for the \u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. Localizing wire enters medially \u000aand exits laterally. Sectioning in the area of the localizing wire \u000ademonstrates firm white fibrodense tissue (30%-40% of specimen), without \u000adefinitive masses, lesions or other abnormalities. Gross calcifications are \u000anot seen. The remaining specimen is predominantly firm fatty tissue without \u000agross abnormality. The entire specimen is submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : entire specimen, sequentially from lateral to medial, \u000amultiple frags, ESS. \u000aPart B is labeled "#2 - right breast outer wire localized biopsy  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ". The \u000aspecimen is received with a radiograph and two localizing wires in place, \u000adesignating an area of calcification marked by a metallic surgical clip, and \u000aconsists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 4.0cm fibrofatty tissue fragment, oriented by \u000asutures designating superior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins. \u000aThe specimen is inked by the pro-sector as follows for the pathology protocol \u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. One wire enters laterally and \u000aexits inferomedially; the second wire enters superolaterally and exits \u000ainferomedially. Sectioning in the area of the localizing wires demonstrates a \u000awhite fibrous tissue, with a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.7cm cavity area filled with \u000atan/white amorphous material, compatible with prior needle core biopsy site. \u000aThis area is 0.2cm from the closest posterior margin and 0.6cm from the \u000aclosest superior margin. Slices are radiographed and the surgical clip is \u000aidentified within the posterior/medial aspect of the specimen. There are no \u000amasses, lesions or other abnormalities grossly. Fibrous tissue comprises \u000aapproximately 70% of the specimen. The remaining tissue is composed of soft, \u000alobulated tan/yellow adipose without gross abnormality. The entire specimen is \u000asubmitted, sequentially from medial/posterior to lateral/anterior, with area of \u000ainterest and surrounding slice in Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . The slice containing \u000aradiologic clip is sectioned and submitted in Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : entire specimen, sequentially from medial/posterior to \u000alateral/anterior, multiple frags, ESS. \u000aDb/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Edilberto G Bauza M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jonathan N Glickman M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a9/14/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
