VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Eric C Huang, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST, 3:00, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A 10 CM AREA OF MRI ENHANCEMENT \u000aASSOCIATED WITH A PALPABLE SUBAREOLAR MASS: \u000aEXTENSIVE ATYPICAL HYPERPLASIA associated with numerous intraductal papillomas \u000awith florid epithelial hyperplasia, present throughout the specimen (completely \u000aexamined microscopically in 38 blocks)(see note). \u000aFocal mucin production is associated with areas of ADH (e.g. block 18; levels \u000aexamined). \u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA with signet ring cells is best seen in the \u000aoriginal level of block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . This area is no longer present on deeper levels \u000aused for immunohistochemical studies. Adjacent areas of ADH are immunoreactive \u000afor estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and e-cadherin, and are negative \u000afor  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu. \u000aMyoepithelial cells are present in papillary areas (immunohistochemical study \u000afor p63; block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aThe palpable mass corresponds to an area of intraductal papillomas. \u000aNote: This is a difficult to classify hyperplasia due to the extensive \u000ainvolvement of papillomas, the florid hyperplasia, and the presence of lobular \u000aneoplasia. In focal areas, micropapillae are formed (e.g.  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aHowever, these areas are insufficient for a diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in \u000asitu. \u000aThe entire specimen is examined microscopically, including margins. \u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Deborah Dillon, who concurs with this \u000ainterpretation. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: History of left nipple discharge with 1.5cm mass at edge of areola at \u000a3 o'clock and abnormal left MRI with abnormal enhancement posterior to nipple. \u000aOperation: Left breast biopsy with MRI guided biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: Not provided. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left nipple discharge. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA. Left breast tissue at 3 o'clock: 2 stitches close together - marks \u000asubareolar mass (please comment on what this is) short = superior, long = \u000alateral \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aMRI loc - please process all tissue. \u000aDensity and ? distortion are in specimen at wire. There is also a palpable \u000afinding in the specimen - possible mammographic correlate is marked "#2". \u000aDr. E.P. Frost \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name and unit number \u000aand consists of a lumpectomy measuring REMOVED_DATE (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE (superior \u000ato inferior) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior)cm with a localization wire. There \u000ais a long suture designated as lateral and a short suture designated as \u000asuperior and a double stitch designated as "subareolar mass" per surgeon. \u000aThe specimen radiograph shows an ill-defined area of tissue density traversed \u000aby the wire, which has been annotated as #1 by the radiologist. This area is \u000ain the lateral portion of the specimen. At the medial end of the specimen, \u000aanother ill-defined density has been circled and annotated by the radiologist \u000aas "#2, ? palpable lesion." \u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: \u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; \u000amedial/orange. The palpable area corresponding to the sutures marking the \u000asubareolar mass measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.8cm and abuts the medial margin, and is \u000a0.3cm from the posterior margin, 1.1cm from the inferior margin, 1.1cm from \u000athe superior margin, 1.5cm from the anterior margin, and > 2.0cm from the \u000alateral margin. The subareolar mass consists of multiple small granular \u000apalpable foci without a discrete mass. \u000aThe ill-defined tissue density in the lateral portion of the specimen measures \u000a2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.1cm  This area is present at the lateral margin, 0.6cm from \u000athe anterior margin, 1.5cm from the posterior margin, 1.2cm from the superior \u000amargin, > 2.0cm from the inferior and medial margins. The specimen consists \u000aof 85% adipose tissue and 15% of fibrous firm tissue. The entire specimen is \u000asubmitted. The gross specimen is reviewed with Dr. Susan Lester of the Breast \u000aPathology Service. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : palpable subareolar mass, medial margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : palpable subareolar mass, anterior, posterior, superior, and \u000ainferior margins, 5 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : tissue between the two densities in the midportion of the \u000aspecimen, anterior, posterior, superior, and inferior margins, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : lateral mass per radiograph, lateral, anterior, posterior, \u000asuperior, and inferior margins, 8 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : remainder of the specimen, 18 frags, ESS. \u000aECH/ajb \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a5/23/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
