VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Consult material \u000aProcedure Date: \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-06- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Peter M Sadow, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aCONSULT SLIDES FROM MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL, BOSTON, MA: \u000aRIGHT BREAST, TWO EXCISIONAL BIOPSIES ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; 9/20/ REMOVED_CASE_ID ): \u000aWIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aEXTENSIVE COLUMNAR CELL CHANGE WITH FLAT ATYPIA, associated with \u000acalcifications, is present in 5 of 7 blocks (see note below). The columnar \u000acell changes are present < 0.1cm from all margins except for the lateral \u000amargin. An en face section of the lateral margin is not involved. \u000aRare micropapillae and a single arch are present in 2 of the 5 blocks (blocks \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; levels examined). \u000aRE-EXCISION: \u000aEXTENSIVE COLUMNAR CELL CHANGE WITH FLAT ATYPIA, associated with \u000acalcifications, is present in 8 of 8 blocks (see note below). By report, the \u000acells are immunoreactive for ER and PR (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). Columnar cell changes are \u000apresent at the posterior, anterior, and inferior margins, < 0.1cm from the \u000asuperior and lateral margins, and REMOVED_DATE to \u000a0.2cm from the medial margin. \u000aRare micropapillae and small cribriform spaces are focally present in 3 of the \u000a8 blocks (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; levels examined). \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present. \u000aNote: Although the columnar cell changes are extensive in both biopsies and \u000aassociated with nuclear atypia, the scant very focal architectural changes \u000a(after complete microscopic examination of both specimens and numerous levels) \u000afavors classifying this lesion as ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA. \u000aSelected slides from this case were also reviewed by Dr. Deborah Dillon and Dr. \u000aStuart Schnitt, who concur with this interpretation. Clinical correlation is \u000asuggested to ensure that all radiologic suspicious calcifications have been \u000aexcised. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: None given \u000aClinical Diagnosis: DCIS \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aConsult slides. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aReceived from Massachusetts General Hospital, Pathology Department, Boston, MA \u000a REMOVED_CASE_ID , are thirty (30) glass slides. \u000aThe slides are labeled " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " and sublabeled " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " through " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", \u000a" REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " through " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " through " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " \u000athrough " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " through " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " through " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", \u000a" REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID " and " REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ", corresponding to A) right breast excisional biopsy (4.0 x \u000a2.0 x 0.7cm ) B) right breast re-excision (4.1 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ) performed on \u000a9/20/06, according to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient's \u000aname. \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a10/11/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Lee K. Tan at Memorial Sloan-Kettering \u000aCancer Center ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; 11/1/ REMOVED_CASE_ID ) and the text of her letter to Dr. Melinda \u000aLerwill is as follows: \u000a?I have now shown this difficult and challenging case to our breast pathology \u000agroup, including Dr. Edi Brogi. As I expected, our opinions range from \u000aextensive atypical columnar cell changes/flat epithelial atypia to ductal \u000acarcinoma in situ. \u000aWhile we all agree that there are foci in these excisions (particularly in the \u000are-excision specimen) that would cytologically qualify for flat/clinging ductal \u000acarcinoma in situ as described by Dr. Azzopardi, we also know that these \u000alesions follow a very indolent clinical course as reported by Eusebi et al. \u000aCoincidentally, I met Dr. Ian Ellis who was at the same meeting I attended a \u000acouple of days ago, and one of his lectures was about columnar cell lesions. \u000aApparently, he and his colleagues have a paper that has been accepted for \u000apublication in the American Journal of Surgical Pathology on this subject. \u000aThey did a molecular study of a spectrum of columnar cell changes including \u000aflat epithelial atypia and low grade (micropapillary) DCIS, and showed ? not \u000asurprisingly ? that there is a continuum. In addition, he also mentioned that \u000awhen a retrospective re-examination of the slides from the European trial of \u000aDCIS, they found cases of DCIS associated with flat epithelial atypia at the \u000aperiphery, with some cases involving margins, and that they did not find an \u000aincreased number of recurrences or increased risk for development of invasive \u000acarcinoma in this subset of patients. Dr. Ellis? observations seem to \u000acorroborate Dr. Eusebi?s publication regarding the indolent biological behavior \u000aof these lesions. \u000aTaking the above findings in consideration and realizing that opinions will \u000avary regarding these borderline epithelial proliferations, our final consensus \u000ais that it is probably best to classify the changes seen in this case as \u000a?columnar cell changes with atypia/flat epithelial atypia.? Given the \u000aextensive nature of the lesion in this case, however, we would recommend \u000are-excision of residual calcifications if clinically indicated and very close \u000aclinical follow-up.? \u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
