VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s) \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Qinghong Yang, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL NODE #1, EXCISION: \u000aTwo lymph nodes with no tumor present. \u000a(Levels examined.) \u000aLEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL NODE #2, EXCISION: \u000aOne lymph node with no tumor present. \u000a(Levels examined.) \u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED FOR CALCIFICATION: \u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with microcalcification. \u000aSclerosing adenosis. \u000aCalcifications in association with benign epithelium. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aLEFT BREAST, RE-EXCISION: \u000aNo residual in situ or invasive carcinoma identified. \u000aSkin with scarring, fat necrosis and foreign body giant cell reaction to suture \u000amaterial. \u000aBreast with biopsy cavity changes. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: A 47-year-old female with left breast cancer, axillary tail, invasive \u000aductal carcinoma; second group of calcifications in left breast needs \u000awire-localized excision. \u000aOperation: \u000a1. Left axillary sentinel node biopsy. \u000a2. Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy. \u000a3. Left breast reexcision lumpectomy. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnoses: 1. Left breast cancer. \u000a2. Left breast calcifications. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Left axillary sentinel node #1. \u000aB/2. Left axillary sentinel node #2. \u000aC/3. Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy, short suture superior, long \u000asuture lateral. \u000aD/4. Left breast reexcision lumpectomy, short suture superior, long lateral. \u000aRADIOLOGY: Ca++ and wire in specimen. RB phoned MA in OR. \u000a(no radiologist's signature) \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in four parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname and unit number. \u000aPart A, "#1. Left axillary sentinel node biopsy #1", consists of one fragment \u000aof yellow/tan to pink/tan fibroadipose tissue (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm ) with focal \u000aareas of blue/green discoloration. The specimen is dissected to exhibit three \u000apink/tan lymph node candidates (ranging from REMOVED_DATE to 1.3cm in greatest \u000adimension). \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 3 lymph node candidates, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, "#2. Left axillary sentinel node biopsy #2", consists of one pink/tan \u000alymph node candidate (0.8cm in greatest dimension) with attached yellow/tan \u000aadipose tissue with focal areas of blue/green discoloration. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : 1 lymph node candidate, 1 frag, ESS. \u000aPart C, "#3. Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy, short suture \u000asuperior, long lateral", consists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to \u000apink/tan fibroadipose tissue (3.4 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm ), which is oriented per \u000asurgeon with two sutures as indicated. The specimen is inked per pathology \u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; \u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one \u000aneedle localization wire in place, which enters the lateral aspect of the \u000aspecimen and exits the anteromedial aspect of the specimen. The specimen was \u000apreviously radiographed. The radiograph shows microcalcifications within the \u000aspecimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit \u000aapproximately 70% diffuse dense/nodular white/tan focally cystic fibrous tissue \u000a(extending to all inked margins), and approximately 30% yellow/tan lobulated \u000aadipose tissue. No discrete masses or lesions are grossly identified. The \u000aspecimen is entirely sequentially submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Central specimen from medial to lateral (area traverse the needle \u000alocalization wire corresponding to calcifications in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 1 frag in each, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart D, "#4. Left breast reexcision lumpectomy, short suture superior, long \u000alateral", consists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibroadipose \u000atissue (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm ), which is oriented per surgeon with two sutures as \u000aindicated. The anterior tip of the specimen exhibits a tan/pink granular skin \u000aellipse (2.1 x 0.8cm ) with a central well-healed linear scar (1.7cm in \u000alength). The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of \u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; \u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. The inferior aspect of the specimen is focally \u000adisrupted (0.9 x 0.6cm ) with associated blue/green dye discoloration. The \u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit focally gritty \u000ayellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue with no discrete masses or lesions \u000aidentified. The specimen is entirely sequentially submitted from medial to \u000alateral. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Central specimen, 3 frags total, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, 4 frags, RSS. \u000aLMB/mmm \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on \u000a5/21/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
