VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Christopher P. Elco, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. LEFT BREAST MEDIAL, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION (RADIOGRAPH SHOWS \u000aCALCIFICATIONS): \u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, associated with calcifications, see NOTE \u000aOther findings: \u000aCalcifications in normal epithelium and apocrine cysts. \u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis. \u000aB. LEFT BREAST LATERAL, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION (RADIOGRAPH SHOWS RARE \u000aCALCIFICATIONS): \u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, associated with calcifications, see NOTE. \u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA, with pagetoid spread. \u000aOther findings: \u000aCalcifications associated with normal epithelium. \u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis. \u000aC. LEFT BREAST MIDDLE, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION (RADIOGRAPH SHOWS CLIP AND \u000aCALCIFICATIONS): \u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, associated with calcifications, see NOTE. \u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA, with pagetoid spread. \u000aOther findings: \u000aCore biopsy site changes \u000aCalcifications associated with normal epithelium. \u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis. \u000aNOTE: The foci of ADH have a solid and cribriform pattern with low nuclear \u000agrade and are present as scattered small foci in several slides in all three \u000aparts. No necrosis is seen associated with these foci. Although a diagnosis of \u000aDCIS has been considered, the atypical proliferations incompletely involve \u000aducts, are of low nuclear grade, and are small foci, and overall it is best \u000aclassified as ADH. Dr. Susan Lester also reviewed selected slides and concurs \u000awith the interpretation. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: Microcalcs left breast. \u000aOperation: Wire directed excision (form 86 page 1 of 2); left breast wire \u000alocalized excisional biopsy (form 86 page 2 of 2). \u000aOperative Findings: See Op Notes. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcs left breast (form 86 page 1 of 2); left breast \u000acalcifications (form 86 page 2 of 2). \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) medial breast mass - left (stitch - long/lateral, short/superior). \u000aB/2) lateral breast mass - left. \u000aC/3) left breast tissue - middle specimen. \u000aRADIOLOGY: \u000aSpecimen #1, Specimen #2 and Specimen #3: Three specimens total with clip, two \u000ahook and wire and calcs. Called Dr. Christian at 12:21 PM on REMOVED_DATE - by Dr. \u000aRaza (similar notes on both pink intra-op notes for radiographs). \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's \u000aname, unit number. \u000aPart A, labeled "medial breast mass-left", consists of an irregular fragment of \u000afibroadipose tissue (5.4cm S-I x 4.2cm M-L x 1.6cm A-P) with a short suture \u000adesignating superior and a long suture designating lateral per surgeon. The \u000aspecimen is received with a needle localization wire entering through the \u000aanterior aspect, with no definitive exit seen on a specimen radiograph. The \u000aradiograph reviewed shows a density and calcifications but no clip present. The \u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol. The specimen is inked by the surgeon \u000aper pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; \u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. \u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior to exhibit a \u000atan-white cavity (0.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm ) located 0.2cm from the superior margin, \u000a0.4cm from the posterior margin, 0.5cm from the anterior margin, 0.9cm from \u000athe lateral margin, 1.6cm from the medial margin, and 2.4cm from the inferior \u000amargin. The needle localization wire passes this area anteriorly. No masses \u000aor lesions are grossly appreciated. The remaining breast parenchyma consists \u000aof 40% dense white fibrous tissue to 60% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. \u000aApproximately 60% of the specimen is submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular superior margin, 4 frags in each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Body sequentially submitted from superior to inferior to include \u000aarea of needle localization wire and biopsy cavity ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 1 frag in each, \u000aRSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular lateral margin, multi frags, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of specimen, superior to inferior, multi frags, ESS. \u000aPart B, labeled "lateral breast mass-left", consists of an irregular unoriented \u000afragment of fibrofatty soft tissue (5.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.8cm ) with a needle \u000alocalization wire traversing the specimen and an accompanying radiograph. The \u000aradiograph reviewed shows rare calcifications but no clip present. The specimen \u000ais serially sectioned to exhibit 98% yellow lobulated homogeneous unremarkable \u000aadipose tissue to 2% wispy white fibrous tissue. No masses or lesions are \u000agrossly appreciated. \u000aAlso received in the same container is a second fragment of irregular lobulated \u000afibroadipose tissue (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm ), which is inked and bisected to \u000aexhibit yellow homogeneous unremarkable lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen \u000ais submitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Larger fragment of fibroadipose tissue to include area designated \u000aby needle localization wire ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), multi frags total, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Additionally received adipose tissue, 2 frags, ESS. \u000aPart C, labeled "left breast tissue, middle specimen", consists of an irregular \u000afragment of fibroadipose tissue (6.5cm M-L x 3.0cm A-P x 1.1cm S-I) with a \u000ashort suture designating superior and a long suture designating lateral per \u000asurgeon. The specimen is received previously inked per pathology protocol. \u000aThe specimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation \u000aof margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; \u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is received with a radiograph, \u000ademonstrating a metallic clip within the specimen and scattered \u000amicrocalcifications. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral \u000ato exhibit an 85% homogeneous yellow lobulated adipose tissue to 15% wispy \u000awhite fibrous tissue. No masses or lesions are grossly appreciated. A \u000ametallic clip cannot be identified within the specimen. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular medial margin, multi frags total, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Body to include area designated by clip per specimen radiograph \u000a( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 1 frag in each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular lateral margin, multi frags in each, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . \u000ajc/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000aMonday REMOVED_MONTH 27,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 04:41:25PM \u000a[report_end] 
