VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Amend/Addenda \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000a********** Addended Report *********** \u000aResident: Kevin B Long, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS: \u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, with a papillary and micropapillary \u000apattern, present in 3 of 15 blocks (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ; levels examined) \u000a(see note). Calcifications are associated with papillary areas of the \u000aADH. \u000aThe ADH is positive for estrogen and progesterone receptor and negative \u000afor  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu. \u000aADH is < 0.1cm from the closest medial margin in two blocks and 0.1cm \u000afrom the inferior margin in one block. ADH is not seen close to the remaining \u000amargins. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies for p63 demonstrate that some myoepithelial cells \u000aare present in the areas of papillary architecture. \u000aNote: The ADH has a micropapillary pattern and approaches a diagnosis of \u000alow grade ductal carcinoma in situ. However, the limited extent of the \u000ainvolved ducts and the preservation of some myoepithelial cells in the areas \u000aof papillary architecture, favor classification as ADH. The presence of \u000athese cells close to margins raises the possibility of an adjacent area of \u000acarcinoma in situ. \u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson, who concurs with \u000athis interpretation. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: New microcalcifications LUOQ on mammo. \u000aOperation: L needle-loc breast biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: None specified. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1) Left breast wire-localized biopsy; short stitch-superior, long-lateral; \u000amammo. \u000aRADIOLOGY: Specimen contains hookwire and calcifications - Amy Tsai MD. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, \u000aand "#1. Left breast wire localized biopsy, short superior, long lateral", and \u000aconsists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan fibroadipose tissue (7.0 x REMOVED_DATE x \u000a2.0cm ), which is oriented per surgeon with two sutures: short equals \u000asuperior, long equals lateral. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol \u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle \u000alocalization wire entering the anterolateral aspect of the specimen with no \u000aclear exit point. The specimen was previously radiographed, confirming the \u000apresence of a hookwire and a cluster of coarse calcifications at the tip of the \u000awire. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral. In the \u000amedial aspect of the breast, there is an ill-defined area of pink/tan gritty \u000afibrous tissue (0.8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm ) within which the tip of the needle \u000alocalization wire is embedded, corresponding to the area of calcifications as \u000aseen on the accompanying radiograph. The ill-defined fibrous area is 0.1cm \u000afrom the medial margin, 1.5cm from the anterior margin, 1.2cm from the \u000ainferior margin, 1.6cm from the superior margin, 2.3cm from the posterior \u000amargin, and 3.5cm from the lateral margin. The remainder of the specimen is \u000acomposed of approximately 15% delicate white/tan fibrous stroma and 85% \u000ayellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue with no additional discrete lesions \u000aidentified. \u000aRepresentative sections (approximately 60% of the specimen) to include all of \u000athe fibrous stroma are submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin to include ill-defined fibrous area/area of wire \u000atip/calcification, multi frags in each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Central specimen, representative sections from medial to lateral \u000ato include additional margins and fibrous stroma, 1 frag in each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Perpendicular sections through lateral margin, 2 frags in each, \u000aRSS. \u000aLM/mmm \u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07- REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a1/10/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: \u000aAn immunoperoxidase study for cytokeratin was also reviewed as part of the \u000aevaluation of this case. \u000aTHE FINAL DIAGNOSIS IS UNCHANGED. \u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
