VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-09- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Andrew M. Quinn, M.D. \u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, TISSUE (10.68 grams): \u000aMinute focus of atypical ductal hyperplasia arising amid a background of \u000acolumnar cell change and cystic changes with apocrine metaplasia. \u000aIntraductal papilloma (0.1cm ) with reactive atypia, less than 0.1cm from the \u000aposterior resection margin. \u000aEntire specimen examined microscopically. \u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen, \u000aincluding margins. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 46 yo woman found on screening mammogram to have new mass, RLI \u000aquadrant; US-guided core biopsy revealed intraductal papilloma with atypia. \u000aOperation: R wire-localized breast bx. \u000aOperative Findings: Not provided. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: R abnormal mammogram. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA. R breast tissue (suture - short/superior; long/lateral) \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh in a container, labeled with the patient's name, \u000amedical record number, and "R breast tissue, suture short superior, long \u000alateral", and consists of a right lumpectomy (10.68 grams) inked by the surgeon \u000aby pathology protocol with red being superior, green being inferior, blue being \u000aanterior, black being posterior, orange being medial, and yellow being lateral. \u000aPlease note that the long stitch has been placed on the medial aspect of the \u000aspecimen, and that Dr. D. Lockhart incorrectly indicated that the stitch had \u000abeen placed laterally. Dr. A. Quinn verified correct ink and stitch \u000aorientation with Dr. D. Lockhart on REMOVED_DATE at 9:05 am. A radiograph on \u000acentricity shows a wire, which enters the inferior aspect of the specimen, \u000aexits the medial aspect of the specimen and localizes a clip as its tip. The \u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior to reveal a firm \u000atan-white fibrous area (1.2cm in greatest dimension), which abuts the \u000aresection margin on its posteromedial aspect, corresponding to the location of \u000athe identified clip. The specimen is 90% fatty and 10% fibrous. The entire \u000aspecimen has been submitted. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior aspect of the specimen, perpendicular sections, 3 frags \u000aeach, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Serial axial sections from superior to inferior (clip in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ), 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Inferior aspect of the specimen, perpendicular section, 4 frags \u000aeach, ESS. \u000aAQU/mmm \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on Wednesday \u000aREMOVED_MONTH 30,  REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 06:57:29PM \u000a[report_end] 
