VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final Type: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE: 26 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON: 26 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 11:44 CLINICAL DATA: Microcalcifications on mammogram. =============================================================================== *** FINAL DIAGNOSIS *** BREAST (RIGHT), EXCISION: 1. INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA, FOCALLY CRIBRIFORM TYPE, CLOSE TO AN INKED MARGIN, WITH SEVERELY ATYPICAL INTRADUCTAL HYPERPLASIA INVOLVING MULTIPLE LOBULES. 2. FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH FOCAL MICROCALCIFICATIONS. Diagnosis by: G. Richard Dickersin, M.D. Signed On: 03 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 14:53 Consulting Pathologist(s): Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. =============================================================================== *** RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum *** RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST (RIGHT): The atypical cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The atypical cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: Intraductal carcinoma cannot be recognized in the sections stained for hormone receptor proteins. One can see atypical ductal cells, however, and they show the same cytological characteristics as the cells that comprise the ductal carcinoma in-situ. In this laboratory, we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way (paraffin sections) and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay. 91% of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. 96% of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections, the predictive value of a positive result was 97% and the predictive value of a negative result was 95%. In this laboratory, we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay. Sixty-five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay, and 98% of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors. THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY. THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES. Added by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 25 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10:47 GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh in the frozen section lab labeled with the patient's named and unit number is a 2.0cm in diameter round, pink-tan fragment of tissue which is serially sectioned to reveal bands of tan tissue surrounded by fat. A needle localizing wire is placed. There is no frozen section done. An accompanying x-ray shows calcifications adjacent to the thick segment of the localization wire. Specimen is submitted entirely in A through E. Resident: Jo-Ann E. Haney, MD *** INTRA-OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS *** FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS(RIGHT BREAST): NO AREAS SUSPICIOUS FOR INFILTRATING CARCINOMA. O.R. Diagnosis by: Andrew Eric Rosenberg, M.D. =============================================================================== SOURCE CARE UNIT: SURGICAL DAY CARE - ACC 3 REPORTS TO: Leslie Wayne Ottinger, M.D. PART : RIGHT BREAST \u000a[report_end] 
