ing the lateral margin and a bowtie stitch designating the superior margin. There is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.0cm gray-white fibrotic cavity containing hemorrhagic soft tissue which is open to the medial margin. The posterior margin is inked black, the anterior margin is inked blue, the medial margin is inked green and the lateral margin is inked yellow. The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm soft tan-pink nodule which abuts the anterior margin and comes to 0.3cm of the medial margin, 0.7cm of the lateral margin, 1.7cm of the posterior margin, 0.8cm of the superior margin and 2.1cm of the inferior margin. Also noted abutting this nodule along the lateral aspect is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm indurated tan-yellow nodule which comes to 0.3cm of the anterior margin, 1.1cm of the posterior margin, 0.7cm of the lateral margin, 0.8cm of the medial margin, 0.8cm of the inferior margin, and greater than 2cm of the superior margin. The remaining cut surface displays focally dense gray-white fibrous tissue with no other lesions or other gross abnormalities. The specimen is entirely submitted from superior to inferior as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , with the superior margin en face as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , the inferior margin en face as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , the first nodule entirely as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and the second nodule entirely as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE: REASON #1: QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Right, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows: The tumor cells stain (> 10% of nuclei stained) Some tumor cells stain (1 to <10% of nuclei stained) Rare tumor cells stain (< 1% of nuclei stained) The tumor cells do not stain (0% of nuclei stained) These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. They have not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test's accuracy and precision. Addendum #1 by Elena F Brachtel M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART : Breast (Right Nipple Duct), Excision [report_end] 
