VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: FinalType: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE: 07 REMOVED_MONTH 97 ACCESSIONED ON: 07 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 13:21 CLINICAL DATA: IOG =============================================================================== *** FINAL DIAGNOSIS *** BREAST (LEFT), EXCISIONAL BIOPSY: INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, GRADE 2, AT LEAST REMOVED_DATE CM., EXTENSIVELY INVADING LYMPHATIC VESSELS AND EXTENDING TO THE MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN ALONG A BROAD FRONT. DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU EXTENDING AWAY FROM THE MASS. SEE NOTE. NOTE: Blood vessel invasion is not seen. Diagnosis by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 11 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 14:10 =============================================================================== *** RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum *** RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Left): The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: In this laboratory, we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way (paraffin sections) and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay. 91% of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. 96% of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections, the predictive value of a positive result was 97% and the predictive value of a negative result was 95%. In this laboratory, we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay. Sixty-five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay, and 98% of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors. Added by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 13 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 13:55 *** HER-2 Addendum *** RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR HER-2/c-erb REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Breast (Left): The tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER-2/c-erb-2. See note. Note: This test was developed and its performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. It has not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test's accuracy and precision. Added by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 04 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 15:32 GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab, labeled with the patient's name and unit number, and "left breast", is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.0cm portion of fibroadipose tissue inked and sectioned to reveal an ill-defined overgrown white firm area measuring 1.2cm and extending to the inked margin. No discrete lesion is grossly identified. No frozen section is done. Representative sections are submitted in cassettes A-J. Resident: DANIELLE ELLIOTT, M.D. *** INTRA-OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS *** GROSS DIAGNOSIS: THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF INFILTRATING CARCIOMA. O.R. Resident: HENRY DONG, MD O.R. Diagnosis by: XXRUFFOLO,EUGENE F. =============================================================================== SOURCE CARE UNIT: SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT -  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO: Susan E Briggs, M.D. PART : BREAST (LEFT), EXCISION 
