VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Updated Type: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report: MS-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE: MS-07- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken: REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit: Same Day Surgery, WACC 3 MEEI MRN: REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service: Breast-1 Results To: KEVIN S. HUGHES, M.D. Resident: Rosalynn M Nazarian, M.D. Signed Out by: Kristina Braaten, M.D. ********** Addended Report *********** Reason for Addendum #1: Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA: Left breast clip and density. FINAL DIAGNOSIS: BREAST (LEFT), EXCISION: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU (SEE TABLE AND NOTE). LOBULAR NEOPLASIA (LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN-SITU). FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH CALCIFICATIONS. SCAR, CONSISTENT WITH PRIOR BIOPSY SITE. NOTE: It is unclear what the correlate to the mammographic density is. There are multiple areas of dense fibrous parenchyma with fibrocystic changes, and the DCIS is present within this background. The DCIS involves the medial aspect of the specimen. TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU TUMOR SIZE: Involving at least 3 of 10 blocks, and spanning at least 1.2cm  GRADE: 1. CALCIFICATIONS: Limited to benign changes. MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU: Positive at the caudal and antero-cephalad margins of the specimen, and just under 0.2cm from the medial margin of the specimen. STAINS FOR RECEPTORS: Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . BREAST (LEFT, ADDITIONAL POSTERIOR TISSUE), EXCISION: FOCAL DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU, GRADE 1, SPANNING REMOVED_DATE CM, PRESENT AT THE UNORIENTED MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN. FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH CALCIFICATIONS. By his/her signature below, the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he/she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses. GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received are two containers, labeled Margaret Scandalis, #323-21-19. Received fresh, labeled "left breast biopsy", is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.7cm fragment of yellow to white lobular breast parenchyma oriented with a long stitch indicating lateral, short stitch indicating anterior, and bowtie indicating cephalad. The specimen contains a needle localization wire to orient the region of a clip and density as seen on the accompanying specimen radiograph. The specimen is inked as follows: Anterior ? blue, posterior ? black, caudal ? yellow, cephalad ? green. The specimen is serially sectioned perpendicular to the medial to lateral long axis to reveal a heterogeneous rubbery fibrous white to yellow breast parenchyma. Foci of hemorrhage are scattered within the fibrous areas. A 0.2cm circular area contains the regions of a clip located 0.6cm from the nearest blue inked margin, 0.9cm from the nearest green inked margin, and 1.3cm from the lateral tip of the specimen. Representative sections of this specimen are submitted as follows: SECTION CODE:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin, radially sectioned.  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Representative sections from medial to lateral, including site of clip and density in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID .  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin, radially sectioned. Note: The specimen was re-examined and no residual discrete lesions identified in the small amount of tissue remaining. Received fresh, labeled "additional posterior tissue", is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.2cm fragment of yellow lobular adipose tissue that is received unoriented. The specimen is entirely black inked, serially sectioned, and submitted in toto in cassettes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Final Diagnosis by Kristina Braaten M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE: REASON #1: QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Left, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows: The tumor cells stain (> 10% of nuclei stained) Some tumor cells stain (1 to <10% of nuclei stained) Rare tumor cells stain (< 1% of nuclei stained) The tumor cells do not stain (0% of nuclei stained) These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. They have not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test's accuracy and precision. Addendum #1 by Elena F Brachtel M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART A: BREAST (LEFT), EXCISION PART B: BREAST (LEFT ADDITIONAL POSTERIOR), EXCISION [report_end] 
