VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: UpdatedType: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report: MS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE: MS-05- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken: REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit: Same Day Surgery Unit Frozen Section Pathologist: None Frozen Section Resident: None Frozen Section Consultant(s): None Consulting Pathologist(s): None Assigned Resident/Path: Michael H Roehrl, M.D., Ph.D. Reports To: MICHELLE C SPECHT, M.D. ********** Addended Report *********** Reason for Addendum #1: Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA: Left breast biopsy with needle localization. Left breast microcalcifications. Family history of breast ca. FINAL DIAGNOSIS: BREAST (LEFT), EXCISION: DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU (SEE TABLE #1 AND SEE NOTE #1). ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, MULTI FOCI, HARBORING MICROCALCIFICATIONS, WITH SEVERAL FOCI BORDERING ON LOW GRADE DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU (SEE NOTE #2) PSEUDOANGIOMATOUS STROMAL HYPERPLASIA. FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES. TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS #1 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU TUMOR SIZE: Involving 1 of 10 blocks and spanning 0.2cm GRADE: 1 CALCIFICATIONS: Limited to benign changes. MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU: Ductal carcinoma in-situ extends to 0.21cm from the inferior margin of the specimen in one focus. STAINS FOR RECEPTORS: Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . NOTE #1: The in-situ carcinoma is e-cadherin positive, thus supporting a diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in-situ rather than (slide a5-1). NOTE #2: A focus of several atypical intraductal hyperplasia that borders on low grade ductal carcinoma in-situ is less than 0.1cm from the superior margin. By his/her signature below, the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he/she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses. GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "left breast biopsy" is a tan-yellow to focally hemorrhagic lumpectomy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.8cm  The specimen is oriented with a short stitch superiorly and a long stitch laterally. From the medial margin of the specimen, two metal guide wires enter the specimen. The long axis of the specimen is medi-lateral. The specimen is inked as follows: Anterior-blue, posterior-black, superior-green, and inferior-yellow. The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.5cm  tan-red, hemorrhagic area 0.4cm from the lateral edge of the specimen that comes to within 0.3cm of the anterior 0.3cm of the inferior 2.5cm from the posterior, and 0.8cm from the superior margin. The hemorrhagic area is surrounded by tan-white, firmer tissue. The specimen is entirely submitted as follows: SECTION CODE:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral tip.  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial tip.  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of the specimen from lateral to medial, microscopically visible calcification, in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Gross Dictation by Michael H Roehrl M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE Micro/Dx Dictation by Michael H Roehrl M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE Final Diagnosis by Dennis Sgroi M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE: REASON: RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Left, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): The in-situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The in-situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. They have not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test's accuracy and precision. Addendum by Atul K Bhan M.D., on REMOVED_DATE Addendum #1 by Atul K Bhan M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_