12 o'clock and a long stitch as the axillary tail. There is a REMOVED_DATE x 4.6cm areola and nipple. The areola has a 3.2cm elliptical surgical scar on the superior aspect. The posterior soft tissue is freely mobile. The specimen is inked as follows: posterior black, anterior blue. The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1.6cm hemorrhagic firm area consistent with previous biopsy site in the superior medial portion of the specimen, posterior to the surgical scar on the areola. The area is 6.2cm from the closest posterior margin, 5.0cm from the superior margin, 7.3cm from the inferior margin, 12.5cm from the lateral margin, and 8.0cm from the medial margin. The previous biopsy site is submitted entirely in C-J. The closest posterior margin is submitted in K. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is grossly unremarkable. Representative sections of the more fibrous areas are submitted in L-M. A lymph node dissection of the axillary tail is performed. Lymph nodes are submitted as follows: N: Four possible nodes. Received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "additional right axillary tissue", is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.9cm unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue. No lymph nodes are grossly palpable. The specimen is serially sectioned, and submitted entirely in O-R. Received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "right breast additional superficial tissue upper outer quadrant", are multiple unoriented fragments of fibroadipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.9cm in aggregate. The specimen is sectioned to reveal no grossly suspicious areas. The fragments are entirely submitted in S-FF. Resident: SARAH ROLLINS, MD *** INTRA-OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS *** FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS #1-2; LYMPH NODE (RIGHT AXILLA): THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY. O.R. Resident: TRACY L. DAVIS, MD., Ph.D. O.R. Diagnosis by: Joseph Misdraji, MD =================================================