V REMOVED_CASE_ID  TOWER,KAY G 48/F <REG REF 01/20> ( REMOVED_CASE_ID ) WIC SCHROEDER,DAVID M.D.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Specimen:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : REMOVED_CASE_ID  SOUT Received: 01/20/06- REMOVED_CASE_ID  Spec Type: - Clinical diagnosis & history: Focal calcifications seen on mammography, benign vs. malignant calcifications Edited by: REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.DORD Gross description: The specimen is received in formalin in one container, labeled "stereotactic breast biopsy, left breast calcification" designated "left breast lower central" and contains a purple cassette which contains two firm, reddish-pink, glistening needle biopsies measuring 0.9cm  in length, 0.3cm  in diameter and 1.5cm  in length, 0.3cm  in diameter. Resubmitted in cassette 1. 2X all. Also present within the container are seven firm, yellowish-pink, glistening needle biopsies ranging in size from 0.4cm  in length x 0.3cm  in diameter to 2.5cm  in length x 0.3cm  in diameter. Submitted in cassettes REMOVED_DATE 7X all. Dictated by: LV Edited by: REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.DORD DIAGNOSIS: Left breast, lower central, needle biopsy: Atypical columnar cell lesion (atypical ductal hyperplasia, columnar cell type) with microcalcifications. Final Diagnosis by: Michael J. Ross, M.D. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.DORD Notes: REMOVED_DATE  All seven slides ret'd from MGH /jam ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) REMOVED_DATE  Dr. Koerner's report rec'd via fax /djd REMOVED_DATE  Sent seven slides (1 1L-3L, 2 2L-3L and 3 2L-3L) to Dr. Frederick Koerner @ MGH via courier for opinion MJR/jam ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) (617) REMOVED_DATE 03/23/06: All six slides and report ret'd from BI Deac Med Center /jam (CR) REMOVED_DATE  Per patient, sent 6 slides to B.I.Deac., Dr. Valerie Staradub, 5th floor, att: Lauren REMOVED_DATE  labeled (1 2L, 1 3L, 2 2L, 2 3L, 3 2L, 3 3L) MJR/dg CO Notes: (Continued) Edited by: REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.GILD REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.DORD REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.MURJ ADDENDUM REPORTAddendum #1 Entered: 04/04/06- REMOVED_CASE_ID  *** ADDITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC INFORMATION *** This report contains an addendum to the original for the purpose of providing additional diagnostic information. The original diagnosis is unchanged. This case has been reviewed by Dr. Frederick Koerner, of the Massachusetts General Hospital, who concurs with the diagnosis. He comments: "I agree wholeheartedly with your analysis of this case. The sections of this core biopsy specimen show a background of slight fibrocystic changes. A population of atypical ductal cells occupies the acini of several terminal duct-lobular units in block "1". The cells appear enlarged and they possess slightly enlarged nuclei, homogeneous chromatin, inconspicuous nucleoli, and modest amounts of eosinophilic, apical cytoplasm. The cells grow in a single, crowded layer; they do not show genuine stratification nor do they form cribriform spaces, trabecular bars, or Roman bridges. Like you, I would classify the proliferation as atypical ductal hyperplasia of columnar cell type. I suspect that the atypical cells represent a neoplastic proliferation, but I cannot exclude the possibility that they constitute an unusual form of ductal hyperplasia. An excision of this region of the patient's left breast would probably clarify this uncertainty."Addendum Final: ROSS,MICHAEL J. M.D. * REMOVED_DATE <04/04/06- REMOVED_CASE_ID > by MD.ROSMJ Edited by: REMOVED_DATE -  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT.MURJCODES:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  - Left breast, NO  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  - Left breast, NO-Atypical  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  - Columnar epithe  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  - Columnar epithe-Lesion, NOS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  - Columnar epithe-Atypical hyperp  REMOVED_ACCESSIO